            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An Exabeam value between slashes may be read as a regular expression; write RGX(\"...\") for a pattern, or drop the slashes for a path."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# exabeam_legacy_regex\n\nAn Exabeam value between slashes may be read as a regular expression; write RGX(\"...\") for a pattern, or drop the slashes for a path.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in an Exabeam Query Language query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by `opentide validate` (its `queries` check) and `opentide validate query`; as a warning or a note, style, by `opentide lint` (its `queries` check). An editor running `opentide lsp start` shows both.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nopentide validate query\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run `opentide validate query` (`--file` for one rule or query file) or `opentide lint --check queries` again. The [Exabeam Query Language reference](https://docs.exabeam.com/en/exa-search/all/search-guide/performing-searches/advanced-search/query-syntax.html) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against.\n",
                "text": "exabeam_legacy_regex\n\nAn Exabeam value between slashes may be read as a regular expression; write RGX(\"...\") for a pattern, or drop the slashes for a path.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in an Exabeam Query Language query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by opentide validate (its queries check) and opentide validate query; as a warning or a note, style, by opentide lint (its queries check). An editor running opentide lsp start shows both.\n\nExample\n\nopentide validate query\n\nHow to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run opentide validate query (--file for one rule or query file) or opentide lint --check queries again. The Exabeam Query Language reference (https://docs.exabeam.com/en/exa-search/all/search-guide/performing-searches/advanced-search/query-syntax.html) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#exabeam_legacy_regex",
              "id": "exabeam_legacy_regex",
              "name": "ExabeamLegacyRegex",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An Exabeam value between slashes may be read as a regular expression; write RGX(\"...\") for a pattern, or drop the slashes for a path."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Exabeam AND and OR are mixed at one level without parentheses; Exabeam does not document their precedence."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# exabeam_mixed_boolean\n\nExabeam AND and OR are mixed at one level without parentheses; Exabeam does not document their precedence.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in an Exabeam Query Language query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by `opentide validate` (its `queries` check) and `opentide validate query`; as a warning or a note, style, by `opentide lint` (its `queries` check). An editor running `opentide lsp start` shows both.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nopentide validate query\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run `opentide validate query` (`--file` for one rule or query file) or `opentide lint --check queries` again. The [Exabeam Query Language reference](https://docs.exabeam.com/en/exa-search/all/search-guide/performing-searches/advanced-search/query-syntax.html) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against.\n",
                "text": "exabeam_mixed_boolean\n\nExabeam AND and OR are mixed at one level without parentheses; Exabeam does not document their precedence.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in an Exabeam Query Language query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by opentide validate (its queries check) and opentide validate query; as a warning or a note, style, by opentide lint (its queries check). An editor running opentide lsp start shows both.\n\nExample\n\nopentide validate query\n\nHow to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run opentide validate query (--file for one rule or query file) or opentide lint --check queries again. The Exabeam Query Language reference (https://docs.exabeam.com/en/exa-search/all/search-guide/performing-searches/advanced-search/query-syntax.html) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#exabeam_mixed_boolean",
              "id": "exabeam_mixed_boolean",
              "name": "ExabeamMixedBoolean",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Exabeam AND and OR are mixed at one level without parentheses; Exabeam does not document their precedence."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An Exabeam rule with the block's name already exists, so deploy did not create a second one."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# exabeam_name_conflict\n\nAn Exabeam rule with the block's name already exists, so deploy did not create a second one.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide deploy` on an `exabeam` block (exabeam-1.0). Before a create, deploy lists the rules whose name holds the block's name (`nameContains`) and compares the names case-insensitively. A match fails the rule, naming the existing rule's id. Deploy never adopts it or records its id.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nexabeam_name_conflict: an Exabeam rule named 'Password spraying from outside the network' already exists (id 016dbc1d-a727-43df-a143-9546dcc81a5a); deploy does not adopt it\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nRename the block (`configurations.exabeam.name`), or delete the old rule in the console. If it is this rule's own earlier create, record its id under `platforms.exabeam.<tenant>` in `.opentide/states/deployment/<uuid>.json` and commit it.\n",
                "text": "exabeam_name_conflict\n\nAn Exabeam rule with the block's name already exists, so deploy did not create a second one.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide deploy on an exabeam block (exabeam-1.0). Before a create, deploy lists the rules whose name holds the block's name (nameContains) and compares the names case-insensitively. A match fails the rule, naming the existing rule's id. Deploy never adopts it or records its id.\n\nExample\n\nexabeam_name_conflict: an Exabeam rule named 'Password spraying from outside the network' already exists (id 016dbc1d-a727-43df-a143-9546dcc81a5a); deploy does not adopt it\n\nHow to fix\n\nRename the block (configurations.exabeam.name), or delete the old rule in the console. If it is this rule's own earlier create, record its id under platforms.exabeam.<tenant> in .opentide/states/deployment/<uuid>.json and commit it."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#exabeam_name_conflict",
              "id": "exabeam_name_conflict",
              "name": "ExabeamNameConflict",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An Exabeam rule with the block's name already exists, so deploy did not create a second one."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Two enabled Exabeam blocks resolve to the same Exabeam rule name."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# exabeam_name_duplicate\n\nTwo enabled Exabeam blocks resolve to the same Exabeam rule name.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide validate` on an `exabeam` block (exabeam-1.0). The Exabeam rule name is the block's `name`, else the rule's `name`, compared case-insensitively. Exabeam's import refuses a rule whose name exists and Search links rules by name, so the second deploy would fail with `exabeam_name_conflict`.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\n# rule-a.yaml and rule-b.yaml, both:\nname: Password spraying from outside the network\nconfigurations:\n  exabeam:\n    enabled: true\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nGive one of the blocks its own `configurations.exabeam.name`, or rename one of the rules.\n",
                "text": "exabeam_name_duplicate\n\nTwo enabled Exabeam blocks resolve to the same Exabeam rule name.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide validate on an exabeam block (exabeam-1.0). The Exabeam rule name is the block's name, else the rule's name, compared case-insensitively. Exabeam's import refuses a rule whose name exists and Search links rules by name, so the second deploy would fail with exabeam_name_conflict.\n\nExample\n\nrule-a.yaml and rule-b.yaml, both:\nname: Password spraying from outside the network\nconfigurations:\n  exabeam:\n    enabled: true\n\nHow to fix\n\nGive one of the blocks its own configurations.exabeam.name, or rename one of the rules."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#exabeam_name_duplicate",
              "id": "exabeam_name_duplicate",
              "name": "ExabeamNameDuplicate",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Two enabled Exabeam blocks resolve to the same Exabeam rule name."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The Exabeam rule name is longer than 128 characters."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# exabeam_name_length\n\nThe Exabeam rule name is longer than 128 characters.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide validate` and `opentide deploy` on an `exabeam` block (exabeam-1.0). The Exabeam rule name is the block's `name`, else the rule's `name`; Exabeam takes at most 128 characters, and deploy never sends a longer one.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nname: A very long rule name that keeps going well past the one hundred and twenty-eight characters an Exabeam rule name may hold, and more\nconfigurations:\n  exabeam:\n    enabled: true\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nSet a shorter `configurations.exabeam.name`, or shorten the rule's `name`.\n",
                "text": "exabeam_name_length\n\nThe Exabeam rule name is longer than 128 characters.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide validate and opentide deploy on an exabeam block (exabeam-1.0). The Exabeam rule name is the block's name, else the rule's name; Exabeam takes at most 128 characters, and deploy never sends a longer one.\n\nExample\n\nname: A very long rule name that keeps going well past the one hundred and twenty-eight characters an Exabeam rule name may hold, and more\nconfigurations:\n  exabeam:\n    enabled: true\n\nHow to fix\n\nSet a shorter configurations.exabeam.name, or shorten the rule's name."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#exabeam_name_length",
              "id": "exabeam_name_length",
              "name": "ExabeamNameLength",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The Exabeam rule name is longer than 128 characters."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Exabeam filters have no != operator; write NOT field:value."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# exabeam_not_equal\n\nExabeam filters have no != operator; write NOT field:value.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in an Exabeam Query Language query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by `opentide validate` (its `queries` check) and `opentide validate query`; as a warning or a note, style, by `opentide lint` (its `queries` check). An editor running `opentide lsp start` shows both.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nopentide validate query\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run `opentide validate query` (`--file` for one rule or query file) or `opentide lint --check queries` again. The [Exabeam Query Language reference](https://docs.exabeam.com/en/exa-search/all/search-guide/performing-searches/advanced-search/query-syntax.html) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against.\n",
                "text": "exabeam_not_equal\n\nExabeam filters have no != operator; write NOT field:value.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in an Exabeam Query Language query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by opentide validate (its queries check) and opentide validate query; as a warning or a note, style, by opentide lint (its queries check). An editor running opentide lsp start shows both.\n\nExample\n\nopentide validate query\n\nHow to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run opentide validate query (--file for one rule or query file) or opentide lint --check queries again. The Exabeam Query Language reference (https://docs.exabeam.com/en/exa-search/all/search-guide/performing-searches/advanced-search/query-syntax.html) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#exabeam_not_equal",
              "id": "exabeam_not_equal",
              "name": "ExabeamNotEqual",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Exabeam filters have no != operator; write NOT field:value."
              }
