              "help": {
                "markdown": "# exabeam_regex\n\nThe Exabeam regular expression is not valid RE2: a syntax error, a back-reference, look-around, an atomic group or a possessive quantifier.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in an Exabeam Query Language query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by `opentide validate` (its `queries` check) and `opentide validate query`; as a warning or a note, style, by `opentide lint` (its `queries` check). An editor running `opentide lsp start` shows both.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nopentide validate query\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run `opentide validate query` (`--file` for one rule or query file) or `opentide lint --check queries` again. The [Exabeam Query Language reference](https://docs.exabeam.com/en/exa-search/all/search-guide/performing-searches/advanced-search/query-syntax.html) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against.\n",
                "text": "exabeam_regex\n\nThe Exabeam regular expression is not valid RE2: a syntax error, a back-reference, look-around, an atomic group or a possessive quantifier.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in an Exabeam Query Language query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by opentide validate (its queries check) and opentide validate query; as a warning or a note, style, by opentide lint (its queries check). An editor running opentide lsp start shows both.\n\nExample\n\nopentide validate query\n\nHow to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run opentide validate query (--file for one rule or query file) or opentide lint --check queries again. The Exabeam Query Language reference (https://docs.exabeam.com/en/exa-search/all/search-guide/performing-searches/advanced-search/query-syntax.html) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#exabeam_regex",
              "id": "exabeam_regex",
              "name": "ExabeamRegex",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The Exabeam regular expression is not valid RE2: a syntax error, a back-reference, look-around, an atomic group or a possessive quantifier."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An Exabeam call for one rule failed."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# exabeam_request_failed\n\nAn Exabeam call for one rule failed.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide deploy` on an `exabeam` block (exabeam-1.0). A call answered an error other than the ones deploy handles itself: not `400` (`exabeam_request_rejected`), not a `404` deploy recovers from, and not a refusal that stops the tenant. The other rules still deploy.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nexabeam_request_failed: PUT /correlation-rules/v2/rules/016dbc1d-a727-43df-a143-9546dcc81a5a: HTTP 502: Bad Gateway\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nDeploy again later. If it keeps failing, check the tenant's status page and the message's details.\n",
                "text": "exabeam_request_failed\n\nAn Exabeam call for one rule failed.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide deploy on an exabeam block (exabeam-1.0). A call answered an error other than the ones deploy handles itself: not 400 (exabeam_request_rejected), not a 404 deploy recovers from, and not a refusal that stops the tenant. The other rules still deploy.\n\nExample\n\nexabeam_request_failed: PUT /correlation-rules/v2/rules/016dbc1d-a727-43df-a143-9546dcc81a5a: HTTP 502: Bad Gateway\n\nHow to fix\n\nDeploy again later. If it keeps failing, check the tenant's status page and the message's details."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#exabeam_request_failed",
              "id": "exabeam_request_failed",
              "name": "ExabeamRequestFailed",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An Exabeam call for one rule failed."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Exabeam refused the rule with HTTP 400."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# exabeam_request_rejected\n\nExabeam refused the rule with HTTP 400.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide deploy` on an `exabeam` block (exabeam-1.0). The answer's `errors[]` name what Exabeam refused (`code`, `message`, `fields`), and its `traceId` identifies the request for Exabeam support.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nexabeam_request_rejected: POST /correlation-rules/v2/rules: HTTP 400: INVALID_QUERY: query is invalid (fields: sequencesConfig.sequences[0].query) (traceId 5f1c…)\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nFix the field the error names, run `opentide validate`, and deploy again. Send the `traceId` to Exabeam support if the rule looks right.\n",
                "text": "exabeam_request_rejected\n\nExabeam refused the rule with HTTP 400.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide deploy on an exabeam block (exabeam-1.0). The answer's errors[] name what Exabeam refused (code, message, fields), and its traceId identifies the request for Exabeam support.\n\nExample\n\nexabeam_request_rejected: POST /correlation-rules/v2/rules: HTTP 400: INVALID_QUERY: query is invalid (fields: sequencesConfig.sequences[0].query) (traceId 5f1c…)\n\nHow to fix\n\nFix the field the error names, run opentide validate, and deploy again. Send the traceId to Exabeam support if the rule looks right."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#exabeam_request_rejected",
              "id": "exabeam_request_rejected",
              "name": "ExabeamRequestRejected",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Exabeam refused the rule with HTTP 400."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An Exabeam AS name is one of the Search guide's reserved words."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# exabeam_reserved_alias\n\nAn Exabeam AS name is one of the Search guide's reserved words.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in an Exabeam Query Language query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by `opentide validate` (its `queries` check) and `opentide validate query`; as a warning or a note, style, by `opentide lint` (its `queries` check). An editor running `opentide lsp start` shows both.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nopentide validate query\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run `opentide validate query` (`--file` for one rule or query file) or `opentide lint --check queries` again. The [Exabeam Query Language reference](https://docs.exabeam.com/en/exa-search/all/search-guide/performing-searches/advanced-search/query-syntax.html) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against.\n",
                "text": "exabeam_reserved_alias\n\nAn Exabeam AS name is one of the Search guide's reserved words.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in an Exabeam Query Language query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by opentide validate (its queries check) and opentide validate query; as a warning or a note, style, by opentide lint (its queries check). An editor running opentide lsp start shows both.\n\nExample\n\nopentide validate query\n\nHow to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run opentide validate query (--file for one rule or query file) or opentide lint --check queries again. The Exabeam Query Language reference (https://docs.exabeam.com/en/exa-search/all/search-guide/performing-searches/advanced-search/query-syntax.html) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#exabeam_reserved_alias",
              "id": "exabeam_reserved_alias",
              "name": "ExabeamReservedAlias",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An Exabeam AS name is one of the Search guide's reserved words."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An Exabeam query has more than three RGX_EXTRACT calls."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# exabeam_rgx_extract_limit\n\nAn Exabeam query has more than three RGX_EXTRACT calls.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in an Exabeam Query Language query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by `opentide validate` (its `queries` check) and `opentide validate query`; as a warning or a note, style, by `opentide lint` (its `queries` check). An editor running `opentide lsp start` shows both.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nopentide validate query\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run `opentide validate query` (`--file` for one rule or query file) or `opentide lint --check queries` again. The [Exabeam Query Language reference](https://docs.exabeam.com/en/exa-search/all/search-guide/performing-searches/advanced-search/query-syntax.html) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against.\n",
                "text": "exabeam_rgx_extract_limit\n\nAn Exabeam query has more than three RGX_EXTRACT calls.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in an Exabeam Query Language query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by opentide validate (its queries check) and opentide validate query; as a warning or a note, style, by opentide lint (its queries check). An editor running opentide lsp start shows both.\n\nExample\n\nopentide validate query\n\nHow to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run opentide validate query (--file for one rule or query file) or opentide lint --check queries again. The Exabeam Query Language reference (https://docs.exabeam.com/en/exa-search/all/search-guide/performing-searches/advanced-search/query-syntax.html) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#exabeam_rgx_extract_limit",
              "id": "exabeam_rgx_extract_limit",
              "name": "ExabeamRgxExtractLimit",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An Exabeam query has more than three RGX_EXTRACT calls."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Exabeam has stopped the rule on its own."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# exabeam_rule_auto_disabled\n\nExabeam has stopped the rule on its own.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by `opentide deploy` on an `exabeam` block (exabeam-1.0). The rule a `PUT` returned has `autoDisabled: true`. Exabeam stops a rule that matches more than 500 times or triggers more than 50 times in five minutes, or whose query or context table is broken.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nexabeam_rule_auto_disabled: Exabeam stopped rule 016dbc1d-a727-43df-a143-9546dcc81a5a (autoDisabled)\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nNarrow the query or raise the threshold, check the context tables it reads, then re-enable the rule in the console.\n",
                "text": "exabeam_rule_auto_disabled\n\nExabeam has stopped the rule on its own.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by opentide deploy on an exabeam block (exabeam-1.0). The rule a PUT returned has autoDisabled: true. Exabeam stops a rule that matches more than 500 times or triggers more than 50 times in five minutes, or whose query or context table is broken.\n\nExample\n\nexabeam_rule_auto_disabled: Exabeam stopped rule 016dbc1d-a727-43df-a143-9546dcc81a5a (autoDisabled)\n\nHow to fix\n\nNarrow the query or raise the threshold, check the context tables it reads, then re-enable the rule in the console."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#exabeam_rule_auto_disabled",
              "id": "exabeam_rule_auto_disabled",
              "name": "ExabeamRuleAutoDisabled",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Exabeam has stopped the rule on its own."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A rule to disable was already gone from Exabeam, so its id was removed."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# exabeam_rule_missing\n\nA rule to disable was already gone from Exabeam, so its id was removed.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by `opentide deploy` on an `exabeam` block (exabeam-1.0). Under `DISABLEMENT`, the `PUT` answered `404`. Deploy creates nothing and removes the binding from `.opentide/states/deployment/`.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nexabeam_rule_missing: the recorded rule no longer exists on the tenant; its id is removed\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nCommit the updated deployment state. Nothing else is needed: the rule is not on the tenant.\n",
                "text": "exabeam_rule_missing\n\nA rule to disable was already gone from Exabeam, so its id was removed.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by opentide deploy on an exabeam block (exabeam-1.0). Under DISABLEMENT, the PUT answered 404. Deploy creates nothing and removes the binding from .opentide/states/deployment/.\n\nExample\n\nexabeam_rule_missing: the recorded rule no longer exists on the tenant; its id is removed\n\nHow to fix\n\nCommit the updated deployment state. Nothing else is needed: the rule is not on the tenant."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#exabeam_rule_missing",
              "id": "exabeam_rule_missing",
              "name": "ExabeamRuleMissing",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A rule to disable was already gone from Exabeam, so its id was removed."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The recorded Exabeam rule was gone, so deploy created it again."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# exabeam_rule_recreated\n\nThe recorded Exabeam rule was gone, so deploy created it again.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by `opentide deploy` on an `exabeam` block (exabeam-1.0). The `PUT` answered `404`: someone deleted the rule in the console. Deploy creates it again (after the name check) and replaces the id in `.opentide/states/deployment/`.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nexabeam_rule_recreated: the recorded rule no longer exists on the tenant; creating it again\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nCommit the updated deployment state. Delete rules through a `DELETION` status, not in the console.\n",
                "text": "exabeam_rule_recreated\n\nThe recorded Exabeam rule was gone, so deploy created it again.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by opentide deploy on an exabeam block (exabeam-1.0). The PUT answered 404: someone deleted the rule in the console. Deploy creates it again (after the name check) and replaces the id in .opentide/states/deployment/.\n\nExample\n\nexabeam_rule_recreated: the recorded rule no longer exists on the tenant; creating it again\n\nHow to fix\n\nCommit the updated deployment state. Delete rules through a DELETION status, not in the console."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#exabeam_rule_recreated",
              "id": "exabeam_rule_recreated",
              "name": "ExabeamRuleRecreated",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The recorded Exabeam rule was gone, so deploy created it again."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A `schedule` recurrence is not one Exabeam takes."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# exabeam_schedule\n\nA `schedule` recurrence is not one Exabeam takes.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide validate` on an `exabeam` block (exabeam-1.0). Each recurrence has `every` (`day`, `week`, `month` or `days`), `from` and `to` as UTC `HH:MMZ`, and exactly the day keys its `every` takes: `week` takes `days` (weekday names), `month` takes `days` (1 to 31) and `last_day`, `days` takes `first` and `last` (weekday names).\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nschedule:\n  - every: week\n    days: [mon]\n    from: \"08:00Z\"\n    to: \"18:00Z\"\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nWrite weekday names in full and lower case (`monday`), times as `H:MMZ` or `HH:MMZ` in quotes, and only the keys the recurrence takes.\n",
                "text": "exabeam_schedule\n\nA schedule recurrence is not one Exabeam takes.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide validate on an exabeam block (exabeam-1.0). Each recurrence has every (day, week, month or days), from and to as UTC HH:MMZ, and exactly the day keys its every takes: week takes days (weekday names), month takes days (1 to 31) and last_day, days takes first and last (weekday names).\n\nExample\n\nschedule:\n  - every: week\n    days: [mon]\n    from: \"08:00Z\"\n    to: \"18:00Z\"\n\nHow to fix\n\nWrite weekday names in full and lower case (monday), times as H:MMZ or HH:MMZ in quotes, and only the keys the recurrence takes."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#exabeam_schedule",
              "id": "exabeam_schedule",
              "name": "ExabeamSchedule",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A `schedule` recurrence is not one Exabeam takes."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The Exabeam rule keeps a schedule the block no longer sets."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# exabeam_schedule_retained\n\nThe Exabeam rule keeps a schedule the block no longer sets.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by `opentide deploy` on an `exabeam` block (exabeam-1.0). The rule a `PUT` returned still has a `scheduleConfig`. Exabeam does not document whether a `PUT` without one clears it, so the rule may still run only at the old times.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nexabeam_schedule_retained: Exabeam rule 016dbc1d-a727-43df-a143-9546dcc81a5a keeps a schedule the block no longer sets; clear it in the console\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nClear the schedule in the Exabeam console, or set `schedule` in the block again.\n",
                "text": "exabeam_schedule_retained\n\nThe Exabeam rule keeps a schedule the block no longer sets.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by opentide deploy on an exabeam block (exabeam-1.0). The rule a PUT returned still has a scheduleConfig. Exabeam does not document whether a PUT without one clears it, so the rule may still run only at the old times.\n\nExample\n\nexabeam_schedule_retained: Exabeam rule 016dbc1d-a727-43df-a143-9546dcc81a5a keeps a schedule the block no longer sets; clear it in the console\n\nHow to fix\n\nClear the schedule in the Exabeam console, or set schedule in the block again."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#exabeam_schedule_retained",
              "id": "exabeam_schedule_retained",
              "name": "ExabeamScheduleRetained",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The Exabeam rule keeps a schedule the block no longer sets."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An Exabeam block has no sequences, or a sequence has no query."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# exabeam_sequences\n\nAn Exabeam block has no sequences, or a sequence has no query.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide validate` on an `exabeam` block (exabeam-1.0). `sequences` is a non-empty list, and every sequence has a non-blank `query`: a filter in the Exabeam Query Language.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nconfigurations:\n  exabeam:\n    sequences:\n      - name: Failed logins\n        query: \"\"\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nWrite the filter in `query`, such as `activity_type:\"app-login\" AND outcome:\"fail\"`, or remove the empty sequence.\n",
                "text": "exabeam_sequences\n\nAn Exabeam block has no sequences, or a sequence has no query.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide validate on an exabeam block (exabeam-1.0). sequences is a non-empty list, and every sequence has a non-blank query: a filter in the Exabeam Query Language.\n\nExample\n\nconfigurations:\n  exabeam:\n    sequences:\n      - name: Failed logins\n        query: \"\"\n\nHow to fix\n\nWrite the filter in query, such as activity_type:\"app-login\" AND outcome:\"fail\", or remove the empty sequence."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#exabeam_sequences",
              "id": "exabeam_sequences",
              "name": "ExabeamSequences",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An Exabeam block has no sequences, or a sequence has no query."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The block `severity` is not `none`, `low`, `medium`, `high` or `critical`."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# exabeam_severity\n\nThe block `severity` is not `none`, `low`, `medium`, `high` or `critical`.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide validate` on an `exabeam` block (exabeam-1.0). Exabeam's severities are lower case. Without `severity`, deploy derives it from `response.alert_severity` (`Informational` is `none`).\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nconfigurations:\n  exabeam:\n    severity: High\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nUse `none`, `low`, `medium`, `high` or `critical`, or remove the key.\n",
                "text": "exabeam_severity\n\nThe block severity is not none, low, medium, high or critical.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide validate on an exabeam block (exabeam-1.0). Exabeam's severities are lower case. Without severity, deploy derives it from response.alert_severity (Informational is none).\n\nExample\n\nconfigurations:\n  exabeam:\n    severity: High\n\nHow to fix\n\nUse none, low, medium, high or critical, or remove the key."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#exabeam_severity",
              "id": "exabeam_severity",
              "name": "ExabeamSeverity",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The block `severity` is not `none`, `low`, `medium`, `high` or `critical`."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Parentheses hold a single Exabeam expression, which the Search guide calls an error; drop them."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# exabeam_single_group\n\nParentheses hold a single Exabeam expression, which the Search guide calls an error; drop them.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in an Exabeam Query Language query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by `opentide validate` (its `queries` check) and `opentide validate query`; as a warning or a note, style, by `opentide lint` (its `queries` check). An editor running `opentide lsp start` shows both.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nopentide validate query\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run `opentide validate query` (`--file` for one rule or query file) or `opentide lint --check queries` again. The [Exabeam Query Language reference](https://docs.exabeam.com/en/exa-search/all/search-guide/performing-searches/advanced-search/query-syntax.html) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against.\n",
                "text": "exabeam_single_group\n\nParentheses hold a single Exabeam expression, which the Search guide calls an error; drop them.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in an Exabeam Query Language query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by opentide validate (its queries check) and opentide validate query; as a warning or a note, style, by opentide lint (its queries check). An editor running opentide lsp start shows both.\n\nExample\n\nopentide validate query\n\nHow to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run opentide validate query (--file for one rule or query file) or opentide lint --check queries again. The Exabeam Query Language reference (https://docs.exabeam.com/en/exa-search/all/search-guide/performing-searches/advanced-search/query-syntax.html) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#exabeam_single_group",
              "id": "exabeam_single_group",
              "name": "ExabeamSingleGroup",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Parentheses hold a single Exabeam expression, which the Search guide calls an error; drop them."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An unquoted number is matched against a field the Exabeam Common Information Model types as a string; Exabeam requires the number in double quotes."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# exabeam_string_field_number\n\nAn unquoted number is matched against a field the Exabeam Common Information Model types as a string; Exabeam requires the number in double quotes.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in an Exabeam Query Language query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by `opentide validate` (its `queries` check) and `opentide validate query`; as a warning or a note, style, by `opentide lint` (its `queries` check). An editor running `opentide lsp start` shows both.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nopentide validate query\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run `opentide validate query` (`--file` for one rule or query file) or `opentide lint --check queries` again. The [Exabeam Query Language reference](https://docs.exabeam.com/en/exa-search/all/search-guide/performing-searches/advanced-search/query-syntax.html) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against.\n",
                "text": "exabeam_string_field_number\n\nAn unquoted number is matched against a field the Exabeam Common Information Model types as a string; Exabeam requires the number in double quotes.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in an Exabeam Query Language query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by opentide validate (its queries check) and opentide validate query; as a warning or a note, style, by opentide lint (its queries check). An editor running opentide lsp start shows both.\n\nExample\n\nopentide validate query\n\nHow to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run opentide validate query (--file for one rule or query file) or opentide lint --check queries again. The Exabeam Query Language reference (https://docs.exabeam.com/en/exa-search/all/search-guide/performing-searches/advanced-search/query-syntax.html) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#exabeam_string_field_number",
              "id": "exabeam_string_field_number",
              "name": "ExabeamStringFieldNumber",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An unquoted number is matched against a field the Exabeam Common Information Model types as a string; Exabeam requires the number in double quotes."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Exabeam refused the tenant's API key, so nothing more is sent to it."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# exabeam_tenant_unauthorized\n\nExabeam refused the tenant's API key, so nothing more is sent to it.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide deploy` on an `exabeam` block (exabeam-1.0). The token request was refused, or a call answered `401` after one new token, or `403`. Deploy stops the tenant. The key needs Correlation Rules at Read Write Delete; `validate query --live` and `opentide query` also need Search, Analyze and Export.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nexabeam_tenant_unauthorized: tenant exabeam-eu refused the API key: HTTP 401\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nCheck the variables `client_id` and `client_secret` name, the tenant's `region` (or `api_url`), and the key's role in the Exabeam console (Settings, API Keys).\n",
                "text": "exabeam_tenant_unauthorized\n\nExabeam refused the tenant's API key, so nothing more is sent to it.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide deploy on an exabeam block (exabeam-1.0). The token request was refused, or a call answered 401 after one new token, or 403. Deploy stops the tenant. The key needs Correlation Rules at Read Write Delete; validate query --live and opentide query also need Search, Analyze and Export.\n\nExample\n\nexabeam_tenant_unauthorized: tenant exabeam-eu refused the API key: HTTP 401\n\nHow to fix\n\nCheck the variables client_id and client_secret name, the tenant's region (or api_url), and the key's role in the Exabeam console (Settings, API Keys)."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#exabeam_tenant_unauthorized",
              "id": "exabeam_tenant_unauthorized",
              "name": "ExabeamTenantUnauthorized",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Exabeam refused the tenant's API key, so nothing more is sent to it."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An Exabeam tenant did not answer, so no rule deploys to it."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# exabeam_tenant_unreachable\n\nAn Exabeam tenant did not answer, so no rule deploys to it.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide deploy` on an `exabeam` block (exabeam-1.0). A request other than a create got no answer (a timeout, a refused connection, a TLS failure), or the token could not be had. Deploy stops the tenant.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nexabeam_tenant_deployment_failed: code=exabeam_tenant_unreachable tenant=exabeam-eu error=exabeam tenant 'exabeam-eu' at https://api.eu.exabeam.cloud sent no answer within 30s\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nCheck the network path to `https://api.<region>.exabeam.cloud`, the tenant's `region` or `api_url`, and the proxy settings, then deploy again.\n",
                "text": "exabeam_tenant_unreachable\n\nAn Exabeam tenant did not answer, so no rule deploys to it.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide deploy on an exabeam block (exabeam-1.0). A request other than a create got no answer (a timeout, a refused connection, a TLS failure), or the token could not be had. Deploy stops the tenant.\n\nExample\n\nexabeam_tenant_deployment_failed: code=exabeam_tenant_unreachable tenant=exabeam-eu error=exabeam tenant 'exabeam-eu' at https://api.eu.exabeam.cloud sent no answer within 30s\n\nHow to fix\n\nCheck the network path to https://api.<region>.exabeam.cloud, the tenant's region or api_url, and the proxy settings, then deploy again."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#exabeam_tenant_unreachable",
              "id": "exabeam_tenant_unreachable",
              "name": "ExabeamTenantUnreachable",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An Exabeam tenant did not answer, so no rule deploys to it."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A threshold's `function` is not an Exabeam function, or its `field` is missing or blank."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# exabeam_threshold_function\n\nA threshold's `function` is not an Exabeam function, or its `field` is missing or blank.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide validate` on an `exabeam` block (exabeam-1.0). `function` is `count`, `unique_count`, `value`, `max`, `min`, `sum` or `average`. Every function but `count` reads a field, so `field` is required; `count` without a field counts events.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nthreshold:\n  function: sum\n  operator: more_than\n  value: 1000000\n  within: 1h\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nSet `field` to the field the function reads (`bytes_out`), or use another function.\n",
                "text": "exabeam_threshold_function\n\nA threshold's function is not an Exabeam function, or its field is missing or blank.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide validate on an exabeam block (exabeam-1.0). function is count, unique_count, value, max, min, sum or average. Every function but count reads a field, so field is required; count without a field counts events.\n\nExample\n\nthreshold:\n  function: sum\n  operator: more_than\n  value: 1000000\n  within: 1h\n\nHow to fix\n\nSet field to the field the function reads (bytes_out), or use another function."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#exabeam_threshold_function",
              "id": "exabeam_threshold_function",
              "name": "ExabeamThresholdFunction",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A threshold's `function` is not an Exabeam function, or its `field` is missing or blank."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A threshold's `operator` is not one its function takes."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# exabeam_threshold_operator\n\nA threshold's `operator` is not one its function takes.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide validate` on an `exabeam` block (exabeam-1.0). `value` takes `in` or `not_in`; every other function takes `more_than` or `less_than`. Exabeam's `between` and `changed` are not supported in this revision: no published rule shows how their value is written.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nthreshold:\n  function: count\n  operator: in\n  value: [5, 10]\n  within: 10m\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nUse `more_than` or `less_than` with a number, or the `value` function with `in` or `not_in`.\n",
                "text": "exabeam_threshold_operator\n\nA threshold's operator is not one its function takes.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide validate on an exabeam block (exabeam-1.0). value takes in or not_in; every other function takes more_than or less_than. Exabeam's between and changed are not supported in this revision: no published rule shows how their value is written.\n\nExample\n\nthreshold:\n  function: count\n  operator: in\n  value: [5, 10]\n  within: 10m\n\nHow to fix\n\nUse more_than or less_than with a number, or the value function with in or not_in."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#exabeam_threshold_operator",
              "id": "exabeam_threshold_operator",
              "name": "ExabeamThresholdOperator",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A threshold's `operator` is not one its function takes."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A threshold's `value` does not have the shape its operator takes."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# exabeam_threshold_value\n\nA threshold's `value` does not have the shape its operator takes.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide validate` on an `exabeam` block (exabeam-1.0). `more_than` and `less_than` compare with a number (`count` and `unique_count`: a non-negative integer); `in` and `not_in` with a non-empty list of strings or numbers. A list item holds no comma, because Exabeam joins the list with commas.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nthreshold:\n  function: count\n  operator: more_than\n  value: 2.5\n  within: 10m\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nWrite a whole number for `count` and `unique_count`, a number for the other numeric functions, and a list for `in` and `not_in`.\n",
                "text": "exabeam_threshold_value\n\nA threshold's value does not have the shape its operator takes.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide validate on an exabeam block (exabeam-1.0). more_than and less_than compare with a number (count and unique_count: a non-negative integer); in and not_in with a non-empty list of strings or numbers. A list item holds no comma, because Exabeam joins the list with commas.\n\nExample\n\nthreshold:\n  function: count\n  operator: more_than\n  value: 2.5\n  within: 10m\n\nHow to fix\n\nWrite a whole number for count and unique_count, a number for the other numeric functions, and a list for in and not_in."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#exabeam_threshold_value",
              "id": "exabeam_threshold_value",
              "name": "ExabeamThresholdValue",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A threshold's `value` does not have the shape its operator takes."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An Exabeam time field (time, approxLogTime, ingest_time, raw_log_time) is matched with an exact value; Search matches time with a range only."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# exabeam_time_exact\n\nAn Exabeam time field (time, approxLogTime, ingest_time, raw_log_time) is matched with an exact value; Search matches time with a range only.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in an Exabeam Query Language query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by `opentide validate` (its `queries` check) and `opentide validate query`; as a warning or a note, style, by `opentide lint` (its `queries` check). An editor running `opentide lsp start` shows both.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nopentide validate query\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run `opentide validate query` (`--file` for one rule or query file) or `opentide lint --check queries` again. The [Exabeam Query Language reference](https://docs.exabeam.com/en/exa-search/all/search-guide/performing-searches/advanced-search/query-syntax.html) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against.\n",
                "text": "exabeam_time_exact\n\nAn Exabeam time field (time, approxLogTime, ingest_time, raw_log_time) is matched with an exact value; Search matches time with a range only.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in an Exabeam Query Language query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by opentide validate (its queries check) and opentide validate query; as a warning or a note, style, by opentide lint (its queries check). An editor running opentide lsp start shows both.\n\nExample\n\nopentide validate query\n\nHow to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run opentide validate query (--file for one rule or query file) or opentide lint --check queries again. The Exabeam Query Language reference (https://docs.exabeam.com/en/exa-search/all/search-guide/performing-searches/advanced-search/query-syntax.html) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#exabeam_time_exact",
              "id": "exabeam_time_exact",
              "name": "ExabeamTimeExact",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An Exabeam time field (time, approxLogTime, ingest_time, raw_log_time) is matched with an exact value; Search matches time with a range only."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The field is not in Exabeam's Common Information Model, a platform field, a c_ or m_ field, or an AS name defined earlier in the query; a tenant's parsers may still define it."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# exabeam_unknown_field\n\nThe field is not in Exabeam's Common Information Model, a platform field, a c_ or m_ field, or an AS name defined earlier in the query; a tenant's parsers may still define it.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in an Exabeam Query Language query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by `opentide validate` (its `queries` check) and `opentide validate query`; as a warning or a note, style, by `opentide lint` (its `queries` check). An editor running `opentide lsp start` shows both.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nopentide validate query\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run `opentide validate query` (`--file` for one rule or query file) or `opentide lint --check queries` again. The [Exabeam Query Language reference](https://docs.exabeam.com/en/exa-search/all/search-guide/performing-searches/advanced-search/query-syntax.html) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against.\n",
                "text": "exabeam_unknown_field\n\nThe field is not in Exabeam's Common Information Model, a platform field, a c_ or m_ field, or an AS name defined earlier in the query; a tenant's parsers may still define it.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in an Exabeam Query Language query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by opentide validate (its queries check) and opentide validate query; as a warning or a note, style, by opentide lint (its queries check). An editor running opentide lsp start shows both.\n\nExample\n\nopentide validate query\n\nHow to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run opentide validate query (--file for one rule or query file) or opentide lint --check queries again. The Exabeam Query Language reference (https://docs.exabeam.com/en/exa-search/all/search-guide/performing-searches/advanced-search/query-syntax.html) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#exabeam_unknown_field",
              "id": "exabeam_unknown_field",
              "name": "ExabeamUnknownField",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The field is not in Exabeam's Common Information Model, a platform field, a c_ or m_ field, or an AS name defined earlier in the query; a tenant's parsers may still define it."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The call is not an Exabeam function: RGX, RGXi, WLD and WLDi in a filter, an aggregation or RGX_EXTRACT in SELECT."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# exabeam_unknown_function\n\nThe call is not an Exabeam function: RGX, RGXi, WLD and WLDi in a filter, an aggregation or RGX_EXTRACT in SELECT.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in an Exabeam Query Language query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by `opentide validate` (its `queries` check) and `opentide validate query`; as a warning or a note, style, by `opentide lint` (its `queries` check). An editor running `opentide lsp start` shows both.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nopentide validate query\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run `opentide validate query` (`--file` for one rule or query file) or `opentide lint --check queries` again. The [Exabeam Query Language reference](https://docs.exabeam.com/en/exa-search/all/search-guide/performing-searches/advanced-search/query-syntax.html) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against.\n",
                "text": "exabeam_unknown_function\n\nThe call is not an Exabeam function: RGX, RGXi, WLD and WLDi in a filter, an aggregation or RGX_EXTRACT in SELECT.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in an Exabeam Query Language query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by opentide validate (its queries check) and opentide validate query; as a warning or a note, style, by opentide lint (its queries check). An editor running opentide lsp start shows both.\n\nExample\n\nopentide validate query\n\nHow to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run opentide validate query (--file for one rule or query file) or opentide lint --check queries again. The Exabeam Query Language reference (https://docs.exabeam.com/en/exa-search/all/search-guide/performing-searches/advanced-search/query-syntax.html) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#exabeam_unknown_function",
              "id": "exabeam_unknown_function",
              "name": "ExabeamUnknownFunction",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The call is not an Exabeam function: RGX, RGXi, WLD and WLDi in a filter, an aggregation or RGX_EXTRACT in SELECT."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An Exabeam `activity_type`, `subject` or `outcome` value is not one the Common Information Model lists."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# exabeam_unknown_value\n\nAn Exabeam `activity_type`, `subject` or `outcome` value is not one the Common Information Model lists.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by the analysis engine in an Exabeam Query Language query (`exabeam`, language-exabeam 1.1), at the value: `opentide lint` (its `queries` check), `opentide validate query` and an editor running `opentide lsp start` show it, and `opentide validate` does not fail on it. A tenant's parsers may emit values the CIM lacks, so it is never an error, but a value no event carries makes the filter match nothing. Values compare without regard to case. Wildcards, `WLD`/`RGX` calls, values between slashes, `null`, an empty value, an unquoted number or IP address, a `@field` and an `AS` name defined earlier are not checked. Engines before language-server 0.16.1 do not report it.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nactivity_type:\"network-connection\" AND process_name:\"powershell.exe\"\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nUse the CIM's value, which the message suggests when one is close (`process_create` → `process-create`) and the editor completes: here `network-session` or `network-traffic`. An `activity_type` is `<subject>-<activity>`. If the tenant really emits the value, confirm it with `opentide query --platform exabeam` and keep it. Then run `opentide lint --check queries` again.\n\n```text\nactivity_type:\"network-session\" AND process_name:\"powershell.exe\"\n```\n",
                "text": "exabeam_unknown_value\n\nAn Exabeam activity_type, subject or outcome value is not one the Common Information Model lists.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by the analysis engine in an Exabeam Query Language query (exabeam, language-exabeam 1.1), at the value: opentide lint (its queries check), opentide validate query and an editor running opentide lsp start show it, and opentide validate does not fail on it. A tenant's parsers may emit values the CIM lacks, so it is never an error, but a value no event carries makes the filter match nothing. Values compare without regard to case. Wildcards, WLD/RGX calls, values between slashes, null, an empty value, an unquoted number or IP address, a @field and an AS name defined earlier are not checked. Engines before language-server 0.16.1 do not report it.\n\nExample\n\nactivity_type:\"network-connection\" AND process_name:\"powershell.exe\"\n\nHow to fix\n\nUse the CIM's value, which the message suggests when one is close (process_create → process-create) and the editor completes: here network-session or network-traffic. An activity_type is <subject>-<activity>. If the tenant really emits the value, confirm it with opentide query --platform exabeam and keep it. Then run opentide lint --check queries again.\n\nactivity_type:\"network-session\" AND process_name:\"powershell.exe\""
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#exabeam_unknown_value",
              "id": "exabeam_unknown_value",
              "name": "ExabeamUnknownValue",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An Exabeam `activity_type`, `subject` or `outcome` value is not one the Common Information Model lists."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An Exabeam field value is a bare word that is not a number, boolean, null, IP address or @field; Exabeam documents string values in double quotes."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# exabeam_unquoted_value\n\nAn Exabeam field value is a bare word that is not a number, boolean, null, IP address or @field; Exabeam documents string values in double quotes.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in an Exabeam Query Language query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by `opentide validate` (its `queries` check) and `opentide validate query`; as a warning or a note, style, by `opentide lint` (its `queries` check). An editor running `opentide lsp start` shows both.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nopentide validate query\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run `opentide validate query` (`--file` for one rule or query file) or `opentide lint --check queries` again. The [Exabeam Query Language reference](https://docs.exabeam.com/en/exa-search/all/search-guide/performing-searches/advanced-search/query-syntax.html) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against.\n",
                "text": "exabeam_unquoted_value\n\nAn Exabeam field value is a bare word that is not a number, boolean, null, IP address or @field; Exabeam documents string values in double quotes.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in an Exabeam Query Language query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by opentide validate (its queries check) and opentide validate query; as a warning or a note, style, by opentide lint (its queries check). An editor running opentide lsp start shows both.\n\nExample\n\nopentide validate query\n\nHow to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run opentide validate query (--file for one rule or query file) or opentide lint --check queries again. The Exabeam Query Language reference (https://docs.exabeam.com/en/exa-search/all/search-guide/performing-searches/advanced-search/query-syntax.html) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#exabeam_unquoted_value",
              "id": "exabeam_unquoted_value",
              "name": "ExabeamUnquotedValue",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An Exabeam field value is a bare word that is not a number, boolean, null, IP address or @field; Exabeam documents string values in double quotes."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A quoted Exabeam value with a space holds * or ?, which Exabeam does not expand inside a phrase."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# exabeam_wildcard_in_phrase\n\nA quoted Exabeam value with a space holds * or ?, which Exabeam does not expand inside a phrase.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in an Exabeam Query Language query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by `opentide validate` (its `queries` check) and `opentide validate query`; as a warning or a note, style, by `opentide lint` (its `queries` check). An editor running `opentide lsp start` shows both.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nopentide validate query\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run `opentide validate query` (`--file` for one rule or query file) or `opentide lint --check queries` again. The [Exabeam Query Language reference](https://docs.exabeam.com/en/exa-search/all/search-guide/performing-searches/advanced-search/query-syntax.html) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against.\n",
                "text": "exabeam_wildcard_in_phrase\n\nA quoted Exabeam value with a space holds * or ?, which Exabeam does not expand inside a phrase.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in an Exabeam Query Language query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by opentide validate (its queries check) and opentide validate query; as a warning or a note, style, by opentide lint (its queries check). An editor running opentide lsp start shows both.\n\nExample\n\nopentide validate query\n\nHow to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run opentide validate query (--file for one rule or query file) or opentide lint --check queries again. The Exabeam Query Language reference (https://docs.exabeam.com/en/exa-search/all/search-guide/performing-searches/advanced-search/query-syntax.html) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#exabeam_wildcard_in_phrase",
              "id": "exabeam_wildcard_in_phrase",
              "name": "ExabeamWildcardInPhrase",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A quoted Exabeam value with a space holds * or ?, which Exabeam does not expand inside a phrase."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A rule still carries a vendor rule id key, which belongs in deployment state."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# external_id_in_rule\n\nA rule still carries a vendor rule id key, which belongs in deployment state.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide validate` (schema check).\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nconfigurations:\n  sentinel:\n    rule_id: 3f2a…           # not a key of the platform block\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nRun `opentide deploy migrate-state` and commit the rule together with `.opentide/states/deployment/`. Vendor ids live in one shard per rule there.\n",
                "text": "external_id_in_rule\n\nA rule still carries a vendor rule id key, which belongs in deployment state.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide validate (schema check).\n\nExample\n\nconfigurations:\n  sentinel:\n    rule_id: 3f2a…           # not a key of the platform block\n\nHow to fix\n\nRun opentide deploy migrate-state and commit the rule together with .opentide/states/deployment/. Vendor ids live in one shard per rule there."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#external_id_in_rule",
              "id": "external_id_in_rule",
              "name": "ExternalIdInRule",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A rule still carries a vendor rule id key, which belongs in deployment state."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A vendor rule id in deployment state has the wrong form."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# external_id_shape\n\nA vendor rule id in deployment state has the wrong form.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide validate` (schema check).\n\n## Example\n\n```json\n{\"object_uuid\": \"…\", \"platforms\": {\"defender_for_endpoint\": {\"prod\": \"-12\"}}}\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nWrite the id the platform returned. Defender and SentinelOne ids are canonical decimal integers (no sign, no leading zeroes); an id is never empty. A deploy writes these shards; edit them by hand only to repair one.\n",
                "text": "external_id_shape\n\nA vendor rule id in deployment state has the wrong form.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide validate (schema check).\n\nExample\n\n{\"object_uuid\": \"…\", \"platforms\": {\"defender_for_endpoint\": {\"prod\": \"-12\"}}}\n\nHow to fix\n\nWrite the id the platform returned. Defender and SentinelOne ids are canonical decimal integers (no sign, no leading zeroes); an id is never empty. A deploy writes these shards; edit them by hand only to repair one."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#external_id_shape",
              "id": "external_id_shape",
              "name": "ExternalIdShape",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A vendor rule id in deployment state has the wrong form."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A tuning's condition is appended to the tuned rule's condition and must start with `and` or `or`; a new rule's condition must not."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# falco_append_condition\n\nA tuning's condition is appended to the tuned rule's condition and must start with `and` or `or`; a new rule's condition must not.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in a Falco condition query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by `opentide validate` (its `queries` check) and `opentide validate query`; as a warning or a note, style, by `opentide lint` (its `queries` check). An editor running `opentide lsp start` shows both.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nopentide validate query\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run `opentide validate query` (`--file` for one rule or query file) or `opentide lint --check queries` again. The [Falco condition reference](https://falco.org/docs/concepts/rules/conditions/) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against.\n",
                "text": "falco_append_condition\n\nA tuning's condition is appended to the tuned rule's condition and must start with and or or; a new rule's condition must not.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in a Falco condition query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by opentide validate (its queries check) and opentide validate query; as a warning or a note, style, by opentide lint (its queries check). An editor running opentide lsp start shows both.\n\nExample\n\nopentide validate query\n\nHow to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run opentide validate query (--file for one rule or query file) or opentide lint --check queries again. The Falco condition reference (https://falco.org/docs/concepts/rules/conditions/) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#falco_append_condition",
              "id": "falco_append_condition",
              "name": "FalcoAppendCondition",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A tuning's condition is appended to the tuned rule's condition and must start with `and` or `or`; a new rule's condition must not."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A field Falco deprecates (`evt.dir`, `evt.latency`): it no longer carries the value it used to."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# falco_deprecated_field\n\nA field Falco deprecates (`evt.dir`, `evt.latency`): it no longer carries the value it used to.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in a Falco condition query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by `opentide validate` (its `queries` check) and `opentide validate query`; as a warning or a note, style, by `opentide lint` (its `queries` check). An editor running `opentide lsp start` shows both.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nopentide validate query\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run `opentide validate query` (`--file` for one rule or query file) or `opentide lint --check queries` again. The [Falco condition reference](https://falco.org/docs/concepts/rules/conditions/) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against.\n",
                "text": "falco_deprecated_field\n\nA field Falco deprecates (evt.dir, evt.latency): it no longer carries the value it used to.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in a Falco condition query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by opentide validate (its queries check) and opentide validate query; as a warning or a note, style, by opentide lint (its queries check). An editor running opentide lsp start shows both.\n\nExample\n\nopentide validate query\n\nHow to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run opentide validate query (--file for one rule or query file) or opentide lint --check queries again. The Falco condition reference (https://falco.org/docs/concepts/rules/conditions/) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#falco_deprecated_field",
              "id": "falco_deprecated_field",
              "name": "FalcoDeprecatedField",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A field Falco deprecates (`evt.dir`, `evt.latency`): it no longer carries the value it used to."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A rule exception Falco refuses: comparison operators, field and value counts, or a value the field's type cannot hold."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# falco_exception\n\nA rule exception Falco refuses: comparison operators, field and value counts, or a value the field's type cannot hold.\n\nReported by the analysis engine on the Sysdig block's `exceptions`, with the condition's findings, since Falco appends them to the condition when it loads the rule: as an error, an exception Falco would reject, by `opentide validate` (its `queries` check); as a warning, by `opentide lint` (its `queries` check). An editor running `opentide lsp start` shows both. An exception whose shape the model refuses (fields, comps and values that do not align) is `sysdig_exception_shape` instead, which `validate` names in its `schema_validation` finding: Falco's checks leave that exception to it.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nopentide validate query\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run `opentide validate query` (`--file` for one rule or query file) or `opentide lint --check queries` again. The [Falco condition reference](https://falco.org/docs/concepts/rules/conditions/) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against.\n",
                "text": "falco_exception\n\nA rule exception Falco refuses: comparison operators, field and value counts, or a value the field's type cannot hold.\n\nReported by the analysis engine on the Sysdig block's exceptions, with the condition's findings, since Falco appends them to the condition when it loads the rule: as an error, an exception Falco would reject, by opentide validate (its queries check); as a warning, by opentide lint (its queries check). An editor running opentide lsp start shows both. An exception whose shape the model refuses (fields, comps and values that do not align) is sysdig_exception_shape instead, which validate names in its schema_validation finding: Falco's checks leave that exception to it.\n\nExample\n\nopentide validate query\n\nHow to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run opentide validate query (--file for one rule or query file) or opentide lint --check queries again. The Falco condition reference (https://falco.org/docs/concepts/rules/conditions/) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#falco_exception",
              "id": "falco_exception",
              "name": "FalcoException",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A rule exception Falco refuses: comparison operators, field and value counts, or a value the field's type cannot hold."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A known Falco field is used with an argument it refuses, or without one it requires (libsinsp's field rules: `proc.aname[N]` takes an index, `evt.arg.NAME` an event argument)."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# falco_field_argument\n\nA known Falco field is used with an argument it refuses, or without one it requires (libsinsp's field rules: `proc.aname[N]` takes an index, `evt.arg.NAME` an event argument).\n\nReported by the analysis engine in a Falco condition query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by `opentide validate` (its `queries` check) and `opentide validate query`; as a warning or a note, style, by `opentide lint` (its `queries` check). An editor running `opentide lsp start` shows both.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nopentide validate query\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run `opentide validate query` (`--file` for one rule or query file) or `opentide lint --check queries` again. The [Falco condition reference](https://falco.org/docs/concepts/rules/conditions/) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against.\n",
                "text": "falco_field_argument\n\nA known Falco field is used with an argument it refuses, or without one it requires (libsinsp's field rules: proc.aname[N] takes an index, evt.arg.NAME an event argument).\n\nReported by the analysis engine in a Falco condition query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by opentide validate (its queries check) and opentide validate query; as a warning or a note, style, by opentide lint (its queries check). An editor running opentide lsp start shows both.\n\nExample\n\nopentide validate query\n\nHow to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run opentide validate query (--file for one rule or query file) or opentide lint --check queries again. The Falco condition reference (https://falco.org/docs/concepts/rules/conditions/) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#falco_field_argument",
              "id": "falco_field_argument",
              "name": "FalcoFieldArgument",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A known Falco field is used with an argument it refuses, or without one it requires (libsinsp's field rules: `proc.aname[N]` takes an index, `evt.arg.NAME` an event argument)."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A constant value that is a field name: compare with `val(field)` to read the field."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# falco_field_as_value\n\nA constant value that is a field name: compare with `val(field)` to read the field.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in a Falco condition query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by `opentide validate` (its `queries` check) and `opentide validate query`; as a warning or a note, style, by `opentide lint` (its `queries` check). An editor running `opentide lsp start` shows both.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nopentide validate query\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run `opentide validate query` (`--file` for one rule or query file) or `opentide lint --check queries` again. The [Falco condition reference](https://falco.org/docs/concepts/rules/conditions/) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against.\n",
                "text": "falco_field_as_value\n\nA constant value that is a field name: compare with val(field) to read the field.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in a Falco condition query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by opentide validate (its queries check) and opentide validate query; as a warning or a note, style, by opentide lint (its queries check). An editor running opentide lsp start shows both.\n\nExample\n\nopentide validate query\n\nHow to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run opentide validate query (--file for one rule or query file) or opentide lint --check queries again. The Falco condition reference (https://falco.org/docs/concepts/rules/conditions/) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#falco_field_as_value",
              "id": "falco_field_as_value",
              "name": "FalcoFieldAsValue",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A constant value that is a field name: compare with `val(field)` to read the field."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The `regex` pattern does not compile."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# falco_invalid_regex\n\nThe `regex` pattern does not compile.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in a Falco condition query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by `opentide validate` (its `queries` check) and `opentide validate query`; as a warning or a note, style, by `opentide lint` (its `queries` check). An editor running `opentide lsp start` shows both.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nopentide validate query\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run `opentide validate query` (`--file` for one rule or query file) or `opentide lint --check queries` again. The [Falco condition reference](https://falco.org/docs/concepts/rules/conditions/) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against.\n",
                "text": "falco_invalid_regex\n\nThe regex pattern does not compile.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in a Falco condition query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by opentide validate (its queries check) and opentide validate query; as a warning or a note, style, by opentide lint (its queries check). An editor running opentide lsp start shows both.\n\nExample\n\nopentide validate query\n\nHow to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run opentide validate query (--file for one rule or query file) or opentide lint --check queries again. The Falco condition reference (https://falco.org/docs/concepts/rules/conditions/) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#falco_invalid_regex",
              "id": "falco_invalid_regex",
              "name": "FalcoInvalidRegex",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The `regex` pattern does not compile."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A list is used where its expansion breaks the condition: as a macro, or as a single value; a list belongs inside a value list, `in (name)`."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# falco_misplaced_list\n\nA list is used where its expansion breaks the condition: as a macro, or as a single value; a list belongs inside a value list, `in (name)`.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in a Falco condition query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by `opentide validate` (its `queries` check) and `opentide validate query`; as a warning or a note, style, by `opentide lint` (its `queries` check). An editor running `opentide lsp start` shows both.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nopentide validate query\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run `opentide validate query` (`--file` for one rule or query file) or `opentide lint --check queries` again. The [Falco condition reference](https://falco.org/docs/concepts/rules/conditions/) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against.\n",
                "text": "falco_misplaced_list\n\nA list is used where its expansion breaks the condition: as a macro, or as a single value; a list belongs inside a value list, in (name).\n\nReported by the analysis engine in a Falco condition query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by opentide validate (its queries check) and opentide validate query; as a warning or a note, style, by opentide lint (its queries check). An editor running opentide lsp start shows both.\n\nExample\n\nopentide validate query\n\nHow to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run opentide validate query (--file for one rule or query file) or opentide lint --check queries again. The Falco condition reference (https://falco.org/docs/concepts/rules/conditions/) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#falco_misplaced_list",
              "id": "falco_misplaced_list",
              "name": "FalcoMisplacedList",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A list is used where its expansion breaks the condition: as a macro, or as a single value; a list belongs inside a value list, `in (name)`."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Syntax that only recent libsinsp releases parse (operator modifiers, `join`, `concat`, `getopt`, transformer arguments); an older Falco or Sysdig agent refuses the rule."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# falco_newer_syntax\n\nSyntax that only recent libsinsp releases parse (operator modifiers, `join`, `concat`, `getopt`, transformer arguments); an older Falco or Sysdig agent refuses the rule.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in a Falco condition query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by `opentide validate` (its `queries` check) and `opentide validate query`; as a warning or a note, style, by `opentide lint` (its `queries` check). An editor running `opentide lsp start` shows both.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nopentide validate query\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run `opentide validate query` (`--file` for one rule or query file) or `opentide lint --check queries` again. The [Falco condition reference](https://falco.org/docs/concepts/rules/conditions/) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against.\n",
                "text": "falco_newer_syntax\n\nSyntax that only recent libsinsp releases parse (operator modifiers, join, concat, getopt, transformer arguments); an older Falco or Sysdig agent refuses the rule.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in a Falco condition query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by opentide validate (its queries check) and opentide validate query; as a warning or a note, style, by opentide lint (its queries check). An editor running opentide lsp start shows both.\n\nExample\n\nopentide validate query\n\nHow to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run opentide validate query (--file for one rule or query file) or opentide lint --check queries again. The Falco condition reference (https://falco.org/docs/concepts/rules/conditions/) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#falco_newer_syntax",
              "id": "falco_newer_syntax",
              "name": "FalcoNewerSyntax",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Syntax that only recent libsinsp releases parse (operator modifiers, `join`, `concat`, `getopt`, transformer arguments); an older Falco or Sysdig agent refuses the rule."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The rule cannot be narrowed to a few event types (`evt.type`), so it runs on every event; Falco and Sysdig warn about the performance penalty."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# falco_no_evttype\n\nThe rule cannot be narrowed to a few event types (`evt.type`), so it runs on every event; Falco and Sysdig warn about the performance penalty.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in a Falco condition query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by `opentide validate` (its `queries` check) and `opentide validate query`; as a warning or a note, style, by `opentide lint` (its `queries` check). An editor running `opentide lsp start` shows both.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nopentide validate query\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run `opentide validate query` (`--file` for one rule or query file) or `opentide lint --check queries` again. The [Falco condition reference](https://falco.org/docs/concepts/rules/conditions/) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against.\n",
                "text": "falco_no_evttype\n\nThe rule cannot be narrowed to a few event types (evt.type), so it runs on every event; Falco and Sysdig warn about the performance penalty.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in a Falco condition query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by opentide validate (its queries check) and opentide validate query; as a warning or a note, style, by opentide lint (its queries check). An editor running opentide lsp start shows both.\n\nExample\n\nopentide validate query\n\nHow to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run opentide validate query (--file for one rule or query file) or opentide lint --check queries again. The Falco condition reference (https://falco.org/docs/concepts/rules/conditions/) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#falco_no_evttype",
              "id": "falco_no_evttype",
              "name": "FalcoNoEvttype",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The rule cannot be narrowed to a few event types (`evt.type`), so it runs on every event; Falco and Sysdig warn about the performance penalty."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The operator does not apply to the field's type (libsinsp `flt_is_comparable`); Falco refuses to load the rule."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# falco_operator_type\n\nThe operator does not apply to the field's type (libsinsp `flt_is_comparable`); Falco refuses to load the rule.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in a Falco condition query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by `opentide validate` (its `queries` check) and `opentide validate query`; as a warning or a note, style, by `opentide lint` (its `queries` check). An editor running `opentide lsp start` shows both.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nopentide validate query\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run `opentide validate query` (`--file` for one rule or query file) or `opentide lint --check queries` again. The [Falco condition reference](https://falco.org/docs/concepts/rules/conditions/) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against.\n",
                "text": "falco_operator_type\n\nThe operator does not apply to the field's type (libsinsp flt_is_comparable); Falco refuses to load the rule.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in a Falco condition query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by opentide validate (its queries check) and opentide validate query; as a warning or a note, style, by opentide lint (its queries check). An editor running opentide lsp start shows both.\n\nExample\n\nopentide validate query\n\nHow to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run opentide validate query (--file for one rule or query file) or opentide lint --check queries again. The Falco condition reference (https://falco.org/docs/concepts/rules/conditions/) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#falco_operator_type",
              "id": "falco_operator_type",
              "name": "FalcoOperatorType",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The operator does not apply to the field's type (libsinsp `flt_is_comparable`); Falco refuses to load the rule."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The output template does not format: a `%` at the end, a length with no field, or a token that is no field or transformer."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# falco_output_syntax\n\nThe output template does not format: a `%` at the end, a length with no field, or a token that is no field or transformer.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in a Falco condition query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by `opentide validate` (its `queries` check) and `opentide validate query`; as a warning or a note, style, by `opentide lint` (its `queries` check). An editor running `opentide lsp start` shows both.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nopentide validate query\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run `opentide validate query` (`--file` for one rule or query file) or `opentide lint --check queries` again. The [Falco condition reference](https://falco.org/docs/concepts/rules/conditions/) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against.\n",
                "text": "falco_output_syntax\n\nThe output template does not format: a % at the end, a length with no field, or a token that is no field or transformer.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in a Falco condition query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by opentide validate (its queries check) and opentide validate query; as a warning or a note, style, by opentide lint (its queries check). An editor running opentide lsp start shows both.\n\nExample\n\nopentide validate query\n\nHow to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run opentide validate query (--file for one rule or query file) or opentide lint --check queries again. The Falco condition reference (https://falco.org/docs/concepts/rules/conditions/) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against."
              },
