            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An object file's name is not the stem `lint.toml` `[filenames].style` (or its kind's `[filenames.<kind>].style`) asks for: `slugify(name)` by default."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# filename_mismatch\n\nAn object file's name is not the stem `lint.toml` `[filenames].style` (or its kind's `[filenames.<kind>].style`) asks for: `slugify(name)` by default.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by `opentide lint` (filenames check).\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nobjects/threats/Simulated Actor.yaml   # name: Simulated Actor → simulated-actor.yaml\n```\n\nWith `style = \"name\"` the stem is the name, less the characters a file name cannot hold (`< > : \" / \\ | ? *`, control characters, and spaces or dots at either end):\n\n```text\nobjects/threats/actor.yaml   # name: Simulated Actor → Simulated Actor.yaml\n```\n\nA stem Windows reserves for a device (`CON`, `PRN`, `AUX`, `NUL`, `CONIN$`, `CONOUT$`, `COM0`-`COM9`, `LPT0`-`LPT9`, in any case and with any extension) gets a `_` before it under either style:\n\n```text\nobjects/rules/con.yaml   # name: CON → _con.yaml\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nRun `opentide lint --fix`, which renames the file. When another file already has the expected name (compared without regard to case), the object gets the first eight characters of its UUID as a suffix.\n\nIf the catalogue keeps another convention, set `style` in `.opentide/configurations/lint.toml`: `\"name\"` for the object's name as the file name, `\"none\"` to stop comparing the stem with the name. `pattern` checks the stem against a regular expression (`opentide explain filename_pattern`). `[filenames.threats]`, `[filenames.objectives]` and `[filenames.rules]` set either key for one kind of object.\n",
                "text": "filename_mismatch\n\nAn object file's name is not the stem lint.toml [filenames].style (or its kind's [filenames.<kind>].style) asks for: slugify(name) by default.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by opentide lint (filenames check).\n\nExample\n\nobjects/threats/Simulated Actor.yaml   # name: Simulated Actor → simulated-actor.yaml\n\nWith style = \"name\" the stem is the name, less the characters a file name cannot hold (< > : \" / \\ | ? *, control characters, and spaces or dots at either end):\n\nobjects/threats/actor.yaml   # name: Simulated Actor → Simulated Actor.yaml\n\nA stem Windows reserves for a device (CON, PRN, AUX, NUL, CONIN$, CONOUT$, COM0-COM9, LPT0-LPT9, in any case and with any extension) gets a _ before it under either style:\n\nobjects/rules/con.yaml   # name: CON → _con.yaml\n\nHow to fix\n\nRun opentide lint --fix, which renames the file. When another file already has the expected name (compared without regard to case), the object gets the first eight characters of its UUID as a suffix.\n\nIf the catalogue keeps another convention, set style in .opentide/configurations/lint.toml: \"name\" for the object's name as the file name, \"none\" to stop comparing the stem with the name. pattern checks the stem against a regular expression (opentide explain filename_pattern). [filenames.threats], [filenames.objectives] and [filenames.rules] set either key for one kind of object."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#filename_mismatch",
              "id": "filename_mismatch",
              "name": "FilenameMismatch",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An object file's name is not the stem `lint.toml` `[filenames].style` (or its kind's `[filenames.<kind>].style`) asks for: `slugify(name)` by default."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An object file's name does not match the regular expression `lint.toml` sets for it."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# filename_pattern\n\nAn object file's name does not match the regular expression `lint.toml` sets for it.\n\nSeverity: warning, or the severity `[filenames]` sets. Reported by `opentide lint` (filenames check), only when `[filenames].pattern`, or the object kind's `[filenames.<kind>].pattern`, is set.\n\n## Example\n\n```toml\n# .opentide/configurations/lint.toml\n[filenames]\nstyle = \"name\"\npattern = '^\\[(SEN|XDR)\\]\\[[^\\]]+\\].+\\[[^\\]]+\\]$'\n```\n\n```text\nobjects/rules/Some rule.yaml   # the stem is not [PLATFORM][GROUP]name[TENANT]\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nThe pattern is matched against the whole file name without its extension (`re.fullmatch`), whatever `style` is. `lint --fix` cannot rename the file, because no name derives from a pattern: rename it so the stem matches, or change `pattern` in `.opentide/configurations/lint.toml` if the convention changed.\n\n`pattern` applies together with `style`. When `style` asks for a stem and `lint --fix` renames the file to it, the pattern is checked against the new name. With `style = \"name\"` the stem is the object's `name`, so a stem that fails the pattern after `--fix` means the `name` itself does not fit it: change the name. With `style = \"none\"` only the pattern is checked.\n",
                "text": "filename_pattern\n\nAn object file's name does not match the regular expression lint.toml sets for it.\n\nSeverity: warning, or the severity [filenames] sets. Reported by opentide lint (filenames check), only when [filenames].pattern, or the object kind's [filenames.<kind>].pattern, is set.\n\nExample\n\n.opentide/configurations/lint.toml\n[filenames]\nstyle = \"name\"\npattern = '^\\[(SEN|XDR)\\]\\[[^\\]]+\\].+\\[[^\\]]+\\]$'\n\nobjects/rules/Some rule.yaml   # the stem is not [PLATFORM][GROUP]name[TENANT]\n\nHow to fix\n\nThe pattern is matched against the whole file name without its extension (re.fullmatch), whatever style is. lint --fix cannot rename the file, because no name derives from a pattern: rename it so the stem matches, or change pattern in .opentide/configurations/lint.toml if the convention changed.\n\npattern applies together with style. When style asks for a stem and lint --fix renames the file to it, the pattern is checked against the new name. With style = \"name\" the stem is the object's name, so a stem that fails the pattern after --fix means the name itself does not fit it: change the name. With style = \"none\" only the pattern is checked."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#filename_pattern",
              "id": "filename_pattern",
              "name": "FilenamePattern",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An object file's name does not match the regular expression `lint.toml` sets for it."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Retired in opentide 0.24.0; `opentide lint` reports a file name that is not the object's as `filename_mismatch`."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# filename_slug\n\nRetired in opentide 0.24.0; `opentide lint` reports a file name that is not the object's as `filename_mismatch`.\n\nSeverity: warning, while it was reported. The file name was not the one `lint.toml`'s `[filenames]` style derives from the object's name.\n\nUntil opentide 0.24.0, the language server reported it as a warning on an object's `name`, with a looser rule than `opentide lint` (any stem that started with the slug passed). The editor now reports what `opentide validate` reports, and `opentide lint` keeps the one file-name rule, `filename_mismatch` (KG-03).\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nopentide lint --check filenames\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nNothing to fix: no command reports this code any more. A suppression list or SARIF baseline that names it can drop it.\n",
                "text": "filename_slug\n\nRetired in opentide 0.24.0; opentide lint reports a file name that is not the object's as filename_mismatch.\n\nSeverity: warning, while it was reported. The file name was not the one lint.toml's [filenames] style derives from the object's name.\n\nUntil opentide 0.24.0, the language server reported it as a warning on an object's name, with a looser rule than opentide lint (any stem that started with the slug passed). The editor now reports what opentide validate reports, and opentide lint keeps the one file-name rule, filename_mismatch (KG-03).\n\nExample\n\nopentide lint --check filenames\n\nHow to fix\n\nNothing to fix: no command reports this code any more. A suppression list or SARIF baseline that names it can drop it."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#filename_slug",
              "id": "filename_slug",
              "name": "FilenameSlug",
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "retired"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Retired in opentide 0.24.0; `opentide lint` reports a file name that is not the object's as `filename_mismatch`."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "git is on `PATH`."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# git\n\ngit is on `PATH`.\n\nReported by `opentide doctor`. Warns when it is missing: `--changed`, the default branch and the CI setup need it.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\n✓ git 2.43.0 · git\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nInstall git from your package manager or https://git-scm.com.\n",
                "text": "git\n\ngit is on PATH.\n\nReported by opentide doctor. Warns when it is missing: --changed, the default branch and the CI setup need it.\n\nExample\n\n✓ git 2.43.0 · git\n\nHow to fix\n\nInstall git from your package manager or https://git-scm.com."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#git",
              "id": "git",
              "name": "Git",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "git is on `PATH`."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The workspace is in a git repository, and its default branch (`origin/HEAD`)."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# git_repository\n\nThe workspace is in a git repository, and its default branch (`origin/HEAD`).\n\nReported by `opentide doctor`. Warns when it is not a repository, or `origin/HEAD` is not set.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\n! ./ is not in a git repository · git_repository\n  → git init ./\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nRun the command it prints: `git init` for a new repository, or `git remote set-head origin --auto` when `origin/HEAD` is unset.\n",
                "text": "git_repository\n\nThe workspace is in a git repository, and its default branch (origin/HEAD).\n\nReported by opentide doctor. Warns when it is not a repository, or origin/HEAD is not set.\n\nExample\n\n! ./ is not in a git repository · git_repository\n  → git init ./\n\nHow to fix\n\nRun the command it prints: git init for a new repository, or git remote set-head origin --auto when origin/HEAD is unset."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#git_repository",
              "id": "git_repository",
              "name": "GitRepository",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The workspace is in a git repository, and its default branch (`origin/HEAD`)."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Google SecOps's compiler refuses a rule in a live query check."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# google_secops_compile_error\n\nGoogle SecOps's compiler refuses a rule in a live query check.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide validate query --platform google_secops --live` (google-secops-1.0). The rule's assembled text is compiled by `verifyRuleText`, which resolves the instance's reference lists and data tables and creates nothing. Each `ERROR` diagnostic is placed on the section key and line it falls in, with Google's message and documentation link.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngoogle_secops_compile_error: configurations.google_secops.condition line 1, column 1: event variable $success is not used\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nFix the section and line the finding names, then run the check again.\n",
                "text": "google_secops_compile_error\n\nGoogle SecOps's compiler refuses a rule in a live query check.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide validate query --platform google_secops --live (google-secops-1.0). The rule's assembled text is compiled by verifyRuleText, which resolves the instance's reference lists and data tables and creates nothing. Each ERROR diagnostic is placed on the section key and line it falls in, with Google's message and documentation link.\n\nExample\n\ngoogle_secops_compile_error: configurations.google_secops.condition line 1, column 1: event variable $success is not used\n\nHow to fix\n\nFix the section and line the finding names, then run the check again."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#google_secops_compile_error",
              "id": "google_secops_compile_error",
              "name": "GoogleSecopsCompileError",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Google SecOps's compiler refuses a rule in a live query check."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Google SecOps's compiler accepts a rule with a warning in a live query check."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# google_secops_compile_warning\n\nGoogle SecOps's compiler accepts a rule with a warning in a live query check.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by `opentide validate query --platform google_secops --live`, and logged by `opentide deploy` (google-secops-1.0): `verifyRuleText` answered `success` with a `WARNING` diagnostic, placed on the section key and line it falls in. The rule still deploys.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngoogle_secops_compile_warning: configurations.google_secops.events line 2, column 5: reference lists are deprecated\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nRead Google's message and its documentation link; change the section when the warning applies.\n",
                "text": "google_secops_compile_warning\n\nGoogle SecOps's compiler accepts a rule with a warning in a live query check.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by opentide validate query --platform google_secops --live, and logged by opentide deploy (google-secops-1.0): verifyRuleText answered success with a WARNING diagnostic, placed on the section key and line it falls in. The rule still deploys.\n\nExample\n\ngoogle_secops_compile_warning: configurations.google_secops.events line 2, column 5: reference lists are deprecated\n\nHow to fix\n\nRead Google's message and its documentation link; change the section when the warning applies."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#google_secops_compile_warning",
              "id": "google_secops_compile_warning",
              "name": "GoogleSecopsCompileWarning",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Google SecOps's compiler accepts a rule with a warning in a live query check."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Google SecOps create answered without a rule id."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# google_secops_create_no_id\n\nA Google SecOps create answered without a rule id.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide deploy` on a `google_secops` tenant (google-secops-1.0). Google answered 2xx with a `name` that does not end in a rule id, or with a body that is not JSON. The `name` of the created rule must end in `rules/ru_<uuid>`, the id deploy records in `.opentide/states/deployment/`. Without it the rule may exist on the instance with no binding.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngoogle_secops_create_no_id: rules.create for password_spray answered name None, which does not end in a rule id\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nFind the rule in Google SecOps (Detection, Rules) by its name. Delete it, or record its `ru_` id under `platforms.google_secops.<tenant>` in the rule's `.opentide/states/deployment/<uuid>.json` and commit it.\n",
                "text": "google_secops_create_no_id\n\nA Google SecOps create answered without a rule id.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide deploy on a google_secops tenant (google-secops-1.0). Google answered 2xx with a name that does not end in a rule id, or with a body that is not JSON. The name of the created rule must end in rules/ru_<uuid>, the id deploy records in .opentide/states/deployment/. Without it the rule may exist on the instance with no binding.\n\nExample\n\ngoogle_secops_create_no_id: rules.create for password_spray answered name None, which does not end in a rule id\n\nHow to fix\n\nFind the rule in Google SecOps (Detection, Rules) by its name. Delete it, or record its ru_ id under platforms.google_secops.<tenant> in the rule's .opentide/states/deployment/<uuid>.json and commit it."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#google_secops_create_no_id",
              "id": "google_secops_create_no_id",
              "name": "GoogleSecopsCreateNoId",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Google SecOps create answered without a rule id."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Google SecOps create got no answer or a server error, so the rule may exist without a binding."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# google_secops_create_unconfirmed\n\nA Google SecOps create got no answer or a server error, so the rule may exist without a binding.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide deploy` on a `google_secops` tenant (google-secops-1.0). The `rules.create` request timed out, its connection was reset, or Google answered a 5xx (`503` included), which can come after the rule was written. Google may have created the rule, but its id never reached opentide, so nothing was recorded, and deploy does not send the create again: a second rule with the same name would follow.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngoogle_secops_create_unconfirmed: the create of rule password_spray got no answer (read timed out)\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nFind the rule in Google SecOps (Detection, Rules) by its name. Delete it and deploy again, or record its `ru_` id under `platforms.google_secops.<tenant>` in the rule's `.opentide/states/deployment/<uuid>.json` and commit it.\n",
                "text": "google_secops_create_unconfirmed\n\nA Google SecOps create got no answer or a server error, so the rule may exist without a binding.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide deploy on a google_secops tenant (google-secops-1.0). The rules.create request timed out, its connection was reset, or Google answered a 5xx (503 included), which can come after the rule was written. Google may have created the rule, but its id never reached opentide, so nothing was recorded, and deploy does not send the create again: a second rule with the same name would follow.\n\nExample\n\ngoogle_secops_create_unconfirmed: the create of rule password_spray got no answer (read timed out)\n\nHow to fix\n\nFind the rule in Google SecOps (Detection, Rules) by its name. Delete it and deploy again, or record its ru_ id under platforms.google_secops.<tenant> in the rule's .opentide/states/deployment/<uuid>.json and commit it."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#google_secops_create_unconfirmed",
              "id": "google_secops_create_unconfirmed",
              "name": "GoogleSecopsCreateUnconfirmed",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Google SecOps create got no answer or a server error, so the rule may exist without a binding."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "opentide has no usable Google credentials for a Google SecOps tenant."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# google_secops_credentials_missing\n\nopentide has no usable Google credentials for a Google SecOps tenant.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide deploy`, `opentide validate query --live` and `opentide query` on a `google_secops` tenant (google-secops-1.0). The tenant's `credentials` variable is unset, names no file, or holds no service-account key; or the tenant names no `credentials` and Application Default Credentials are not set up.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngoogle_secops_credentials_missing: the credentials variable GOOGLE_SECOPS_CREDENTIALS is not set\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nSet the variable to a service-account key (its JSON, or the path to the file), or leave `credentials` out and set up Application Default Credentials (`gcloud auth application-default login`, or workload identity in CI).\n",
                "text": "google_secops_credentials_missing\n\nopentide has no usable Google credentials for a Google SecOps tenant.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide deploy, opentide validate query --live and opentide query on a google_secops tenant (google-secops-1.0). The tenant's credentials variable is unset, names no file, or holds no service-account key; or the tenant names no credentials and Application Default Credentials are not set up.\n\nExample\n\ngoogle_secops_credentials_missing: the credentials variable GOOGLE_SECOPS_CREDENTIALS is not set\n\nHow to fix\n\nSet the variable to a service-account key (its JSON, or the path to the file), or leave credentials out and set up Application Default Credentials (gcloud auth application-default login, or workload identity in CI)."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#google_secops_credentials_missing",
              "id": "google_secops_credentials_missing",
              "name": "GoogleSecopsCredentialsMissing",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "opentide has no usable Google credentials for a Google SecOps tenant."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Google SecOps refused to delete a rule, so deploy archived it."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# google_secops_delete_refused\n\nGoogle SecOps refused to delete a rule, so deploy archived it.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by `opentide deploy` on a `google_secops` tenant with `deletion = \"delete\"` (google-secops-1.0). `DELETE` without `force` fails when the rule has detections or retrohunts. Google does not document the status it answers, so any 4xx but 401, 403, 404 and 429 counts as a refusal, and the warning carries Google's message. opentide never sends `force=true`, which deletes the detections too. The rule was disabled and archived instead, and its binding kept.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngoogle_secops_delete_refused: Google refused to delete rule ru_cfc80c6b-f918-42ed-8d5c-9518c13586c1 because it has detections or retrohunts; it is archived instead\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nNothing to do: an archived rule keeps its detections as the audit trail of what it found. Delete it in the console when they are no longer needed.\n",
                "text": "google_secops_delete_refused\n\nGoogle SecOps refused to delete a rule, so deploy archived it.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by opentide deploy on a google_secops tenant with deletion = \"delete\" (google-secops-1.0). DELETE without force fails when the rule has detections or retrohunts. Google does not document the status it answers, so any 4xx but 401, 403, 404 and 429 counts as a refusal, and the warning carries Google's message. opentide never sends force=true, which deletes the detections too. The rule was disabled and archived instead, and its binding kept.\n\nExample\n\ngoogle_secops_delete_refused: Google refused to delete rule ru_cfc80c6b-f918-42ed-8d5c-9518c13586c1 because it has detections or retrohunts; it is archived instead\n\nHow to fix\n\nNothing to do: an archived rule keeps its detections as the audit trail of what it found. Delete it in the console when they are no longer needed."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#google_secops_delete_refused",
              "id": "google_secops_delete_refused",
              "name": "GoogleSecopsDeleteRefused",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Google SecOps refused to delete a rule, so deploy archived it."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Google SecOps rule exists, but its run state could not be set."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# google_secops_deployment_failed\n\nA Google SecOps rule exists, but its run state could not be set.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide deploy` on a `google_secops` tenant (google-secops-1.0). The rule text was created or updated and its id is recorded, but the update of its deployment (`enabled`, `alerting`, `runFrequency`, `archived`) failed. The binding is kept: the next deploy repairs the run state.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngoogle_secops_deployment_failed: rule ru_cfc80c6b-f918-42ed-8d5c-9518c13586c1 is bound, but its run state could not be set\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nRead Google's error in the message (a permission such as `chronicle.ruleDeployments.update`, a run frequency) and deploy again.\n",
                "text": "google_secops_deployment_failed\n\nA Google SecOps rule exists, but its run state could not be set.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide deploy on a google_secops tenant (google-secops-1.0). The rule text was created or updated and its id is recorded, but the update of its deployment (enabled, alerting, runFrequency, archived) failed. The binding is kept: the next deploy repairs the run state.\n\nExample\n\ngoogle_secops_deployment_failed: rule ru_cfc80c6b-f918-42ed-8d5c-9518c13586c1 is bound, but its run state could not be set\n\nHow to fix\n\nRead Google's error in the message (a permission such as chronicle.ruleDeployments.update, a run frequency) and deploy again."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#google_secops_deployment_failed",
              "id": "google_secops_deployment_failed",
              "name": "GoogleSecopsDeploymentFailed",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Google SecOps rule exists, but its run state could not be set."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Google SecOps rule with two or more event variables asks for `run_frequency: LIVE`."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# google_secops_live_frequency_unavailable\n\nA Google SecOps rule with two or more event variables asks for `run_frequency: LIVE`.\n\nSeverity: warning (an advisory). Reported by `opentide validate` and `opentide lint` on a `google_secops` block (google-secops-1.0). Google allows `LIVE` for single-event rules only: `verifyRuleText` does not list it for this rule, so deploy refuses it with `google_secops_run_frequency_not_allowed` before writing anything. Google also counts a single event variable with `match` and a count or outcome condition as multi-event; the advisory does not try to decide that, and deploy still refuses.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nconfigurations:\n  google_secops:\n    run_frequency: LIVE\n    events: |\n      $fail.metadata.event_type = \"USER_LOGIN\"\n      $success.metadata.event_type = \"USER_LOGIN\"\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nWrite `run_frequency: LIVE_CUSTOMIZABLE`, or remove `run_frequency`: deploy then sends none, and Google gives the rule its default frequency.\n",
                "text": "google_secops_live_frequency_unavailable\n\nA Google SecOps rule with two or more event variables asks for run_frequency: LIVE.\n\nSeverity: warning (an advisory). Reported by opentide validate and opentide lint on a google_secops block (google-secops-1.0). Google allows LIVE for single-event rules only: verifyRuleText does not list it for this rule, so deploy refuses it with google_secops_run_frequency_not_allowed before writing anything. Google also counts a single event variable with match and a count or outcome condition as multi-event; the advisory does not try to decide that, and deploy still refuses.\n\nExample\n\nconfigurations:\n  google_secops:\n    run_frequency: LIVE\n    events: |\n      $fail.metadata.event_type = \"USER_LOGIN\"\n      $success.metadata.event_type = \"USER_LOGIN\"\n\nHow to fix\n\nWrite run_frequency: LIVE_CUSTOMIZABLE, or remove run_frequency: deploy then sends none, and Google gives the rule its default frequency."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#google_secops_live_frequency_unavailable",
              "id": "google_secops_live_frequency_unavailable",
              "name": "GoogleSecopsLiveFrequencyUnavailable",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Google SecOps rule with two or more event variables asks for `run_frequency: LIVE`."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Google SecOps block's `meta` holds a key deploy cannot write."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# google_secops_meta_key\n\nA Google SecOps block's `meta` holds a key deploy cannot write.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide validate` (its `schema` check) on a `google_secops` block (google-secops-1.0). Each extra meta key is written after the derived meta as `key = \"value\"`: it must be an identifier that is not a YARA-L keyword, it must not be a key deploy derives (`author`, `description`, `severity`, `priority`, `technique`, `opentide_uuid`, `opentide_version`), and its value is a string. The finding is on `meta.<key>`.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nconfigurations:\n  google_secops:\n    meta:\n      severity: Low        # deploy writes severity itself\n      data_source: Okta\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nRemove the derived key (set `severity` on the block instead) or rename it; quote a value YAML would read as a number or a boolean.\n",
                "text": "google_secops_meta_key\n\nA Google SecOps block's meta holds a key deploy cannot write.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide validate (its schema check) on a google_secops block (google-secops-1.0). Each extra meta key is written after the derived meta as key = \"value\": it must be an identifier that is not a YARA-L keyword, it must not be a key deploy derives (author, description, severity, priority, technique, opentide_uuid, opentide_version), and its value is a string. The finding is on meta.<key>.\n\nExample\n\nconfigurations:\n  google_secops:\n    meta:\n      severity: Low        # deploy writes severity itself\n      data_source: Okta\n\nHow to fix\n\nRemove the derived key (set severity on the block instead) or rename it; quote a value YAML would read as a number or a boolean."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#google_secops_meta_key",
              "id": "google_secops_meta_key",
              "name": "GoogleSecopsMetaKey",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Google SecOps block's `meta` holds a key deploy cannot write."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Two enabled Google SecOps blocks resolve to one YARA-L rule name."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# google_secops_name_duplicate\n\nTwo enabled Google SecOps blocks resolve to one YARA-L rule name.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide validate` (its `schema` check) on a `google_secops` block (google-secops-1.0). The rule name is the block's `name`, else the rule's title lower-cased with runs of other characters as `_`. Names are compared case-insensitively: a composite rule that matches a producer by `detection.detection.rule_name` would match both, and Google's own tooling treats names as unique regardless of case.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\n# rule-a.yaml: name: Password spray\n# rule-b.yaml:\nconfigurations:\n  google_secops:\n    name: PASSWORD_SPRAY\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nGive one of the blocks its own `configurations.google_secops.name`, or rename one of the rules.\n",
                "text": "google_secops_name_duplicate\n\nTwo enabled Google SecOps blocks resolve to one YARA-L rule name.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide validate (its schema check) on a google_secops block (google-secops-1.0). The rule name is the block's name, else the rule's title lower-cased with runs of other characters as _. Names are compared case-insensitively: a composite rule that matches a producer by detection.detection.rule_name would match both, and Google's own tooling treats names as unique regardless of case.\n\nExample\n\nrule-a.yaml: name: Password spray\nrule-b.yaml:\nconfigurations:\n  google_secops:\n    name: PASSWORD_SPRAY\n\nHow to fix\n\nGive one of the blocks its own configurations.google_secops.name, or rename one of the rules."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#google_secops_name_duplicate",
              "id": "google_secops_name_duplicate",
              "name": "GoogleSecopsNameDuplicate",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Two enabled Google SecOps blocks resolve to one YARA-L rule name."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Chronicle API request for one Google SecOps rule failed."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# google_secops_request_failed\n\nA Chronicle API request for one Google SecOps rule failed.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide deploy` on a `google_secops` tenant (google-secops-1.0). Google answered an error other than the ones deploy handles itself, or stayed rate limited (`RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED` or HTTP 503) after three waits: `Retry-After` (seconds or a date, at most 120 seconds), else the `RetryInfo` delay Google names (at most 120 seconds), else 5, 15 and 60 seconds. The message carries Google's `error.status` and `error.message`. The other rules still deploy.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngoogle_secops_request_failed: INVALID_ARGUMENT: the rule text is too long\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nRead Google's status and message; deploy again once the cause is fixed.\n",
                "text": "google_secops_request_failed\n\nA Chronicle API request for one Google SecOps rule failed.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide deploy on a google_secops tenant (google-secops-1.0). Google answered an error other than the ones deploy handles itself, or stayed rate limited (RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED or HTTP 503) after three waits: Retry-After (seconds or a date, at most 120 seconds), else the RetryInfo delay Google names (at most 120 seconds), else 5, 15 and 60 seconds. The message carries Google's error.status and error.message. The other rules still deploy.\n\nExample\n\ngoogle_secops_request_failed: INVALID_ARGUMENT: the rule text is too long\n\nHow to fix\n\nRead Google's status and message; deploy again once the cause is fixed."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#google_secops_request_failed",
              "id": "google_secops_request_failed",
              "name": "GoogleSecopsRequestFailed",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Chronicle API request for one Google SecOps rule failed."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Google SecOps rule's `outcome` assigns no `$risk_score`."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# google_secops_risk_score_missing\n\nA Google SecOps rule's `outcome` assigns no `$risk_score`.\n\nSeverity: warning (an advisory: the rule still validates and deploys). Reported by `opentide validate` and `opentide lint` (its `queries` check) on a `google_secops` block (google-secops-1.0). Without `$risk_score`, Google gives every detection of the rule the instance's default score, 40 for an alert and 15 otherwise unless the instance's settings change them, whatever it detects.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nconfigurations:\n  google_secops:\n    outcome: |\n      $host = $e.principal.hostname\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nAdd a `$risk_score` line to `outcome`, an integer from 0 to 100 (`$risk_score = 70`), or an aggregation in a rule with `match` (`$risk_score = max(70)`).\n",
                "text": "google_secops_risk_score_missing\n\nA Google SecOps rule's outcome assigns no $risk_score.\n\nSeverity: warning (an advisory: the rule still validates and deploys). Reported by opentide validate and opentide lint (its queries check) on a google_secops block (google-secops-1.0). Without $risk_score, Google gives every detection of the rule the instance's default score, 40 for an alert and 15 otherwise unless the instance's settings change them, whatever it detects.\n\nExample\n\nconfigurations:\n  google_secops:\n    outcome: |\n      $host = $e.principal.hostname\n\nHow to fix\n\nAdd a $risk_score line to outcome, an integer from 0 to 100 ($risk_score = 70), or an aggregation in a rule with match ($risk_score = max(70))."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#google_secops_risk_score_missing",
              "id": "google_secops_risk_score_missing",
              "name": "GoogleSecopsRiskScoreMissing",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Google SecOps rule's `outcome` assigns no `$risk_score`."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Google reports that a deployed Google SecOps rule no longer compiles."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# google_secops_rule_compilation_failed\n\nGoogle reports that a deployed Google SecOps rule no longer compiles.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by `opentide deploy` on a `google_secops` tenant (google-secops-1.0), from the rule it reads before an update. `compilationState` `FAILED` happens after a backwards-incompatible change to Google's compiler, or when a reference list or data table the rule uses is gone; the rule then does not run. Deploy goes on: a changed text is verified and sent as usual.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngoogle_secops_rule_compilation_failed: Google reports the rule's compilation as FAILED\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nRun `opentide validate query --platform google_secops --live` to see Google's diagnostics for the rule, fix the sections they name, and deploy again.\n",
                "text": "google_secops_rule_compilation_failed\n\nGoogle reports that a deployed Google SecOps rule no longer compiles.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by opentide deploy on a google_secops tenant (google-secops-1.0), from the rule it reads before an update. compilationState FAILED happens after a backwards-incompatible change to Google's compiler, or when a reference list or data table the rule uses is gone; the rule then does not run. Deploy goes on: a changed text is verified and sent as usual.\n\nExample\n\ngoogle_secops_rule_compilation_failed: Google reports the rule's compilation as FAILED\n\nHow to fix\n\nRun opentide validate query --platform google_secops --live to see Google's diagnostics for the rule, fix the sections they name, and deploy again."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#google_secops_rule_compilation_failed",
              "id": "google_secops_rule_compilation_failed",
              "name": "GoogleSecopsRuleCompilationFailed",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Google reports that a deployed Google SecOps rule no longer compiles."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Google SecOps rule changed between deploy's read and its update."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# google_secops_rule_conflict\n\nA Google SecOps rule changed between deploy's read and its update.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide deploy` on a `google_secops` tenant (google-secops-1.0). The text update carried the `etag` of the rule deploy had just read, and Google refused it (HTTP 409, `ABORTED`): someone or something changed the rule in between. Nothing was written, and deploy does not retry in the same run.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngoogle_secops_rule_conflict: rule ru_cfc80c6b-f918-42ed-8d5c-9518c13586c1 changed between its read and the update (etag mismatch)\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nDeploy again. When it keeps happening, find what else edits the rule in Google SecOps.\n",
                "text": "google_secops_rule_conflict\n\nA Google SecOps rule changed between deploy's read and its update.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide deploy on a google_secops tenant (google-secops-1.0). The text update carried the etag of the rule deploy had just read, and Google refused it (HTTP 409, ABORTED): someone or something changed the rule in between. Nothing was written, and deploy does not retry in the same run.\n\nExample\n\ngoogle_secops_rule_conflict: rule ru_cfc80c6b-f918-42ed-8d5c-9518c13586c1 changed between its read and the update (etag mismatch)\n\nHow to fix\n\nDeploy again. When it keeps happening, find what else edits the rule in Google SecOps."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#google_secops_rule_conflict",
              "id": "google_secops_rule_conflict",
              "name": "GoogleSecopsRuleConflict",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Google SecOps rule changed between deploy's read and its update."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Google reports that it limits or pauses a Google SecOps rule's runs."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# google_secops_rule_execution_limited\n\nGoogle reports that it limits or pauses a Google SecOps rule's runs.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by `opentide deploy` on a `google_secops` tenant (google-secops-1.0), from the rule deployment it reads before an update. `executionState` `LIMITED` means Google does not guarantee the rule's runs, usually because it is expensive or produces too many detections; `PAUSED` means Google does not run it at all. Deploy goes on: the text and run state are still updated.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngoogle_secops_rule_execution_limited: Google reports the rule's execution as PAUSED: it is not run at all\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nOpen the rule in Google SecOps (Detection, Rules) to see why. Narrow `events` (filter on `metadata.event_type` or `metadata.log_type`), shorten the `match` window, or reduce the detections it produces, then deploy again.\n",
                "text": "google_secops_rule_execution_limited\n\nGoogle reports that it limits or pauses a Google SecOps rule's runs.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by opentide deploy on a google_secops tenant (google-secops-1.0), from the rule deployment it reads before an update. executionState LIMITED means Google does not guarantee the rule's runs, usually because it is expensive or produces too many detections; PAUSED means Google does not run it at all. Deploy goes on: the text and run state are still updated.\n\nExample\n\ngoogle_secops_rule_execution_limited: Google reports the rule's execution as PAUSED: it is not run at all\n\nHow to fix\n\nOpen the rule in Google SecOps (Detection, Rules) to see why. Narrow events (filter on metadata.event_type or metadata.log_type), shorten the match window, or reduce the detections it produces, then deploy again."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#google_secops_rule_execution_limited",
              "id": "google_secops_rule_execution_limited",
              "name": "GoogleSecopsRuleExecutionLimited",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Google reports that it limits or pauses a Google SecOps rule's runs."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Google SecOps rule matches a producer rule by a literal `ru_…` id."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# google_secops_rule_id_literal\n\nA Google SecOps rule matches a producer rule by a literal `ru_…` id.\n\nSeverity: warning (an advisory). Reported by `opentide validate` and `opentide lint` on a `google_secops` block (google-secops-1.0). A rule id differs on every instance and changes when the rule is recreated, so a composite rule that compares `detection.detection.rule_id` with a literal stops matching after a redeploy or on another tenant.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nconfigurations:\n  google_secops:\n    events: |\n      $d.detection.detection.rule_id = \"ru_cfc80c6b-f918-42ed-8d5c-9518c13586c1\"\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nMatch the producer by its name, which opentide derives the same way on every instance: `$d.detection.detection.rule_name = \"password_spray_then_success\"`.\n",
                "text": "google_secops_rule_id_literal\n\nA Google SecOps rule matches a producer rule by a literal ru_… id.\n\nSeverity: warning (an advisory). Reported by opentide validate and opentide lint on a google_secops block (google-secops-1.0). A rule id differs on every instance and changes when the rule is recreated, so a composite rule that compares detection.detection.rule_id with a literal stops matching after a redeploy or on another tenant.\n\nExample\n\nconfigurations:\n  google_secops:\n    events: |\n      $d.detection.detection.rule_id = \"ru_cfc80c6b-f918-42ed-8d5c-9518c13586c1\"\n\nHow to fix\n\nMatch the producer by its name, which opentide derives the same way on every instance: $d.detection.detection.rule_name = \"password_spray_then_success\"."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#google_secops_rule_id_literal",
              "id": "google_secops_rule_id_literal",
              "name": "GoogleSecopsRuleIdLiteral",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Google SecOps rule matches a producer rule by a literal `ru_…` id."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The Google SecOps rule a binding names no longer exists, and the rule is being retired."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# google_secops_rule_missing\n\nThe Google SecOps rule a binding names no longer exists, and the rule is being retired.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by `opentide deploy` on a `google_secops` tenant (google-secops-1.0). Under `DISABLEMENT` or `DELETION`, reading or updating the recorded rule answered 404. Deploy created nothing and removed the binding.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngoogle_secops_rule_missing: the recorded rule no longer exists on the instance; its id is removed\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nNothing to do: commit `.opentide/states/deployment/`.\n",
                "text": "google_secops_rule_missing\n\nThe Google SecOps rule a binding names no longer exists, and the rule is being retired.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by opentide deploy on a google_secops tenant (google-secops-1.0). Under DISABLEMENT or DELETION, reading or updating the recorded rule answered 404. Deploy created nothing and removed the binding.\n\nExample\n\ngoogle_secops_rule_missing: the recorded rule no longer exists on the instance; its id is removed\n\nHow to fix\n\nNothing to do: commit .opentide/states/deployment/."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#google_secops_rule_missing",
              "id": "google_secops_rule_missing",
              "name": "GoogleSecopsRuleMissing",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The Google SecOps rule a binding names no longer exists, and the rule is being retired."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Google SecOps block's `name` is not a YARA-L rule name."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# google_secops_rule_name\n\nA Google SecOps block's `name` is not a YARA-L rule name.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide validate` (its `schema` check) on a `google_secops` block (google-secops-1.0). The name is written as `rule <name> {`: it must be an identifier, `[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*`, and not a YARA-L keyword such as `match` or `rule`. An empty `name` is derived from the rule's title instead.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nconfigurations:\n  google_secops:\n    name: password-spray   # a hyphen is not allowed\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nWrite the name with letters, digits and underscores (`password_spray`), or remove `name` to use the one derived from the rule's title.\n",
                "text": "google_secops_rule_name\n\nA Google SecOps block's name is not a YARA-L rule name.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide validate (its schema check) on a google_secops block (google-secops-1.0). The name is written as rule <name> {: it must be an identifier, [A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*, and not a YARA-L keyword such as match or rule. An empty name is derived from the rule's title instead.\n\nExample\n\nconfigurations:\n  google_secops:\n    name: password-spray   # a hyphen is not allowed\n\nHow to fix\n\nWrite the name with letters, digits and underscores (password_spray), or remove name to use the one derived from the rule's title."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#google_secops_rule_name",
              "id": "google_secops_rule_name",
              "name": "GoogleSecopsRuleName",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Google SecOps block's `name` is not a YARA-L rule name."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The Google SecOps rule a binding names no longer exists, so deploy created it again."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# google_secops_rule_recreated\n\nThe Google SecOps rule a binding names no longer exists, so deploy created it again.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by `opentide deploy` on a `google_secops` tenant (google-secops-1.0). Reading or updating the recorded rule answered 404. Deploy verified and created the rule again and replaced the binding with the new id. Detections of the old rule are not carried over.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngoogle_secops_rule_recreated: the recorded rule no longer exists on the instance; creating it again\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nNothing to do: commit `.opentide/states/deployment/` with the new id. Find out who deleted the rule if that was not intended.\n",
                "text": "google_secops_rule_recreated\n\nThe Google SecOps rule a binding names no longer exists, so deploy created it again.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by opentide deploy on a google_secops tenant (google-secops-1.0). Reading or updating the recorded rule answered 404. Deploy verified and created the rule again and replaced the binding with the new id. Detections of the old rule are not carried over.\n\nExample\n\ngoogle_secops_rule_recreated: the recorded rule no longer exists on the instance; creating it again\n\nHow to fix\n\nNothing to do: commit .opentide/states/deployment/ with the new id. Find out who deleted the rule if that was not intended."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#google_secops_rule_recreated",
              "id": "google_secops_rule_recreated",
              "name": "GoogleSecopsRuleRecreated",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The Google SecOps rule a binding names no longer exists, so deploy created it again."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Google SecOps block's `run_frequency` is not one opentide deploys."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# google_secops_run_frequency\n\nA Google SecOps block's `run_frequency` is not one opentide deploys.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide validate` (its `schema` check) on a `google_secops` block (google-secops-1.0). `run_frequency` is `LIVE`, `LIVE_CUSTOMIZABLE`, `HOURLY_CUSTOMIZABLE` or `DAILY`. Google's legacy `HOURLY` is refused: Google remaps it, so the stored rule would never match the block.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nconfigurations:\n  google_secops:\n    run_frequency: HOURLY\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nWrite `HOURLY_CUSTOMIZABLE` (or another of the four values), or remove `run_frequency`: deploy then sends none, and Google keeps the rule's frequency, or gives a new rule its default.\n",
                "text": "google_secops_run_frequency\n\nA Google SecOps block's run_frequency is not one opentide deploys.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide validate (its schema check) on a google_secops block (google-secops-1.0). run_frequency is LIVE, LIVE_CUSTOMIZABLE, HOURLY_CUSTOMIZABLE or DAILY. Google's legacy HOURLY is refused: Google remaps it, so the stored rule would never match the block.\n\nExample\n\nconfigurations:\n  google_secops:\n    run_frequency: HOURLY\n\nHow to fix\n\nWrite HOURLY_CUSTOMIZABLE (or another of the four values), or remove run_frequency: deploy then sends none, and Google keeps the rule's frequency, or gives a new rule its default."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#google_secops_run_frequency",
              "id": "google_secops_run_frequency",
              "name": "GoogleSecopsRunFrequency",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Google SecOps block's `run_frequency` is not one opentide deploys."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Google SecOps block's `run_frequency` is not one Google allows for the rule."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# google_secops_run_frequency_not_allowed\n\nA Google SecOps block's `run_frequency` is not one Google allows for the rule.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide deploy` before any write, and by `opentide validate query --platform google_secops --live` (google-secops-1.0). `verifyRuleText` answers the frequencies the rule can run at (`allowedRunFrequencies`): `LIVE` is for single-event rules, `LIVE_CUSTOMIZABLE` for multi-event windows under an hour, `HOURLY_CUSTOMIZABLE` up to two days, `DAILY` beyond.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngoogle_secops_run_frequency_not_allowed: run_frequency LIVE is not one Google allows for this rule (LIVE_CUSTOMIZABLE, HOURLY_CUSTOMIZABLE, DAILY); nothing was written\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nSet `run_frequency` to one of the frequencies the message lists, or remove it: deploy then sends no `runFrequency`, and Google keeps the rule's frequency, or gives a new rule its default.\n",
                "text": "google_secops_run_frequency_not_allowed\n\nA Google SecOps block's run_frequency is not one Google allows for the rule.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide deploy before any write, and by opentide validate query --platform google_secops --live (google-secops-1.0). verifyRuleText answers the frequencies the rule can run at (allowedRunFrequencies): LIVE is for single-event rules, LIVE_CUSTOMIZABLE for multi-event windows under an hour, HOURLY_CUSTOMIZABLE up to two days, DAILY beyond.\n\nExample\n\ngoogle_secops_run_frequency_not_allowed: run_frequency LIVE is not one Google allows for this rule (LIVE_CUSTOMIZABLE, HOURLY_CUSTOMIZABLE, DAILY); nothing was written\n\nHow to fix\n\nSet run_frequency to one of the frequencies the message lists, or remove it: deploy then sends no runFrequency, and Google keeps the rule's frequency, or gives a new rule its default."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#google_secops_run_frequency_not_allowed",
              "id": "google_secops_run_frequency_not_allowed",
              "name": "GoogleSecopsRunFrequencyNotAllowed",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Google SecOps block's `run_frequency` is not one Google allows for the rule."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Google SecOps block's `schedule` cannot be sent with its run frequency, or holds a duration that is not one."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# google_secops_schedule\n\nA Google SecOps block's `schedule` cannot be sent with its run frequency, or holds a duration that is not one.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide validate` (its `schema` check) on a `google_secops` block (google-secops-1.0). Schedule customizations apply to `LIVE_CUSTOMIZABLE` and `HOURLY_CUSTOMIZABLE` rules only, and `late_arriving_data_adjustment` is a positive duration with unit `s`, `m`, `h` or `d` (`30m`, `2h`). The finding is on `schedule`, or on `schedule.late_arriving_data_adjustment`.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nconfigurations:\n  google_secops:\n    run_frequency: LIVE\n    schedule:\n      late_arriving_data_adjustment: half an hour\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nSet `run_frequency` to `LIVE_CUSTOMIZABLE` or `HOURLY_CUSTOMIZABLE`, or remove `schedule`; write the adjustment as a duration such as `30m`.\n",
                "text": "google_secops_schedule\n\nA Google SecOps block's schedule cannot be sent with its run frequency, or holds a duration that is not one.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide validate (its schema check) on a google_secops block (google-secops-1.0). Schedule customizations apply to LIVE_CUSTOMIZABLE and HOURLY_CUSTOMIZABLE rules only, and late_arriving_data_adjustment is a positive duration with unit s, m, h or d (30m, 2h). The finding is on schedule, or on schedule.late_arriving_data_adjustment.\n\nExample\n\nconfigurations:\n  google_secops:\n    run_frequency: LIVE\n    schedule:\n      late_arriving_data_adjustment: half an hour\n\nHow to fix\n\nSet run_frequency to LIVE_CUSTOMIZABLE or HOURLY_CUSTOMIZABLE, or remove schedule; write the adjustment as a duration such as 30m."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#google_secops_schedule",
              "id": "google_secops_schedule",
              "name": "GoogleSecopsSchedule",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Google SecOps block's `schedule` cannot be sent with its run frequency, or holds a duration that is not one."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Google SecOps block's `severity` or `priority` is not one of Google's values."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# google_secops_severity\n\nA Google SecOps block's `severity` or `priority` is not one of Google's values.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide validate` (its `schema` check) on a `google_secops` block (google-secops-1.0). Both are written into the rule's meta and must be `Info`, `Low`, `Medium`, `High` or `Critical`, Google's community spelling. `Informational` is opentide's alert severity, not Google's.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nconfigurations:\n  google_secops:\n    severity: Informational\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nUse `Info`, `Low`, `Medium`, `High` or `Critical`, or remove the key: `severity` is derived from `response.alert_severity`, and `priority` defaults to the severity.\n",
                "text": "google_secops_severity\n\nA Google SecOps block's severity or priority is not one of Google's values.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide validate (its schema check) on a google_secops block (google-secops-1.0). Both are written into the rule's meta and must be Info, Low, Medium, High or Critical, Google's community spelling. Informational is opentide's alert severity, not Google's.\n\nExample\n\nconfigurations:\n  google_secops:\n    severity: Informational\n\nHow to fix\n\nUse Info, Low, Medium, High or Critical, or remove the key: severity is derived from response.alert_severity, and priority defaults to the severity."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#google_secops_severity",
              "id": "google_secops_severity",
              "name": "GoogleSecopsSeverity",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Google SecOps block's `severity` or `priority` is not one of Google's values."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Google SecOps instance refused opentide's credentials."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# google_secops_tenant_unauthorized\n\nA Google SecOps instance refused opentide's credentials.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide deploy` and `opentide validate query --live` on a `google_secops` tenant (google-secops-1.0). The instance answered HTTP 401 or 403, or no access token could be obtained (Google refused the credentials, or its token endpoint did not answer): no rule on that tenant is deployed or checked. A token failure happens before the request is sent, so it is never an unanswered create. User credentials from Application Default Credentials send their quota project (`x-goog-user-project`); Google refuses them without one.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngoogle_secops_tenant_unauthorized: tenant secops-eu refused the request (HTTP 403 PERMISSION_DENIED): Permission 'chronicle.rules.create' denied\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nGive the service account (or the Application Default Credentials principal) the permissions on the instance: `chronicle.rules.verifyRuleText`, `.create`, `.get`, `.update`, `.delete`, `chronicle.ruleDeployments.get`, `.update`, and `chronicle.events.udmSearch` for `opentide query`. Check `project`, `location` and `instance` in `platforms/google_secops.toml`. With user Application Default Credentials, set a quota project: `gcloud auth application-default set-quota-project <project>`.\n",
                "text": "google_secops_tenant_unauthorized\n\nA Google SecOps instance refused opentide's credentials.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide deploy and opentide validate query --live on a google_secops tenant (google-secops-1.0). The instance answered HTTP 401 or 403, or no access token could be obtained (Google refused the credentials, or its token endpoint did not answer): no rule on that tenant is deployed or checked. A token failure happens before the request is sent, so it is never an unanswered create. User credentials from Application Default Credentials send their quota project (x-goog-user-project); Google refuses them without one.\n\nExample\n\ngoogle_secops_tenant_unauthorized: tenant secops-eu refused the request (HTTP 403 PERMISSION_DENIED): Permission 'chronicle.rules.create' denied\n\nHow to fix\n\nGive the service account (or the Application Default Credentials principal) the permissions on the instance: chronicle.rules.verifyRuleText, .create, .get, .update, .delete, chronicle.ruleDeployments.get, .update, and chronicle.events.udmSearch for opentide query. Check project, location and instance in platforms/google_secops.toml. With user Application Default Credentials, set a quota project: gcloud auth application-default set-quota-project <project>."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#google_secops_tenant_unauthorized",
              "id": "google_secops_tenant_unauthorized",
              "name": "GoogleSecopsTenantUnauthorized",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Google SecOps instance refused opentide's credentials."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Google SecOps instance did not answer, so no rule was deployed to it."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# google_secops_tenant_unreachable\n\nA Google SecOps instance did not answer, so no rule was deployed to it.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide deploy` on a `google_secops` tenant (google-secops-1.0). A request to the instance's endpoint failed in transit (DNS, TLS, a refused or reset connection, a timeout). The tenant stops; the others go on.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngoogle_secops_tenant_unreachable: google_secops tenant 'eu' at https://eu-chronicle.googleapis.com sent no answer within 30s\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nCheck the tenant's `location` (or `endpoint`), the network and proxy between the runner and Google, and `ssl`; then deploy again.\n",
                "text": "google_secops_tenant_unreachable\n\nA Google SecOps instance did not answer, so no rule was deployed to it.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide deploy on a google_secops tenant (google-secops-1.0). A request to the instance's endpoint failed in transit (DNS, TLS, a refused or reset connection, a timeout). The tenant stops; the others go on.\n\nExample\n\ngoogle_secops_tenant_unreachable: google_secops tenant 'eu' at https://eu-chronicle.googleapis.com sent no answer within 30s\n\nHow to fix\n\nCheck the tenant's location (or endpoint), the network and proxy between the runner and Google, and ssl; then deploy again."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#google_secops_tenant_unreachable",
              "id": "google_secops_tenant_unreachable",
              "name": "GoogleSecopsTenantUnreachable",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Google SecOps instance did not answer, so no rule was deployed to it."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Google SecOps does not compile a rule, so deploy neither creates nor updates it."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# google_secops_verify_failed\n\nGoogle SecOps does not compile a rule, so deploy neither creates nor updates it.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide deploy` on a `google_secops` tenant (google-secops-1.0). Deploy sends the assembled rule to `verifyRuleText` before every create and every text update. Each of Google's diagnostics is reported at the section key and line it falls in (`configurations.google_secops.events line 3, column 5`), or on the block when it falls in the generated header or meta.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngoogle_secops_verify_failed: Google SecOps does not compile rule password_spray: configurations.google_secops.events line 3, column 5: field principal.nope does not exist\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nFix the section the diagnostic names and run `opentide validate query --platform google_secops --live`, which asks the same compiler without deploying.\n",
                "text": "google_secops_verify_failed\n\nGoogle SecOps does not compile a rule, so deploy neither creates nor updates it.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide deploy on a google_secops tenant (google-secops-1.0). Deploy sends the assembled rule to verifyRuleText before every create and every text update. Each of Google's diagnostics is reported at the section key and line it falls in (configurations.google_secops.events line 3, column 5), or on the block when it falls in the generated header or meta.\n\nExample\n\ngoogle_secops_verify_failed: Google SecOps does not compile rule password_spray: configurations.google_secops.events line 3, column 5: field principal.nope does not exist\n\nHow to fix\n\nFix the section the diagnostic names and run opentide validate query --platform google_secops --live, which asks the same compiler without deploying."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#google_secops_verify_failed",
              "id": "google_secops_verify_failed",
              "name": "GoogleSecopsVerifyFailed",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Google SecOps does not compile a rule, so deploy neither creates nor updates it."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog aggregation condition does not parse, or a call has the wrong arguments."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_aggregation\n\nA Graylog aggregation condition does not parse, or a call has the wrong arguments.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide validate` on a `graylog` block (graylog-1.0), at `aggregation.condition` (or `aggregation` when it has no condition). The condition compares series with numbers: `count() >= 3 OR card(parent_image_lc) >= 2`. The functions are `avg`, `card`, `count`, `latest`, `max`, `min`, `percentage`, `percentile`, `stddev`, `sum`, `sumofsquares` and `variance`; `count` takes no field or one, `percentile` a field and a percentile from above 0 to 100, every other function one field. Operators `AND`, `OR` and `NOT` are upper case. Arguments are separated by one comma (`percentile(bytes, 95)`), and parentheses and `NOT` nest at most 32 deep.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    aggregation:\n      condition: count() >= 3 or avg() > 1\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nWrite the condition in the grammar: `count() >= 3 OR avg(bytes) > 1`.\n",
                "text": "graylog_aggregation\n\nA Graylog aggregation condition does not parse, or a call has the wrong arguments.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide validate on a graylog block (graylog-1.0), at aggregation.condition (or aggregation when it has no condition). The condition compares series with numbers: count() >= 3 OR card(parent_image_lc) >= 2. The functions are avg, card, count, latest, max, min, percentage, percentile, stddev, sum, sumofsquares and variance; count takes no field or one, percentile a field and a percentile from above 0 to 100, every other function one field. Operators AND, OR and NOT are upper case. Arguments are separated by one comma (percentile(bytes, 95)), and parentheses and NOT nest at most 32 deep.\n\nExample\n\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    aggregation:\n      condition: count() >= 3 or avg() > 1\n\nHow to fix\n\nWrite the condition in the grammar: count() >= 3 OR avg(bytes) > 1."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_aggregation",
              "id": "graylog_aggregation",
              "name": "GraylogAggregation",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog aggregation condition does not parse, or a call has the wrong arguments."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog block names a stream or notification alias the tenant does not define."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_alias_unknown\n\nA Graylog block names a stream or notification alias the tenant does not define.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide deploy` on a `graylog` block (graylog-1.0), before any call for that rule, and by `opentide deploy --dry-run`. Blocks name streams and notifications by alias; each tenant maps the aliases to ids in `stream_ids` and `notification_ids`.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngraylog_alias_unknown: tenant 'graylog-prod': stream alias(es) sysmon are not in the tenant's stream_ids table\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nAdd the alias to the tenant's table in `platforms/graylog.toml`, or change the block.\n",
                "text": "graylog_alias_unknown\n\nA Graylog block names a stream or notification alias the tenant does not define.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide deploy on a graylog block (graylog-1.0), before any call for that rule, and by opentide deploy --dry-run. Blocks name streams and notifications by alias; each tenant maps the aliases to ids in stream_ids and notification_ids.\n\nExample\n\ngraylog_alias_unknown: tenant 'graylog-prod': stream alias(es) sysmon are not in the tenant's stream_ids table\n\nHow to fix\n\nAdd the alias to the tenant's table in platforms/graylog.toml, or change the block."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_alias_unknown",
              "id": "graylog_alias_unknown",
              "name": "GraylogAliasUnknown",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog block names a stream or notification alias the tenant does not define."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog definition names no stream, so it searches every stream."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_all_streams\n\nA Graylog definition names no stream, so it searches every stream.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by `opentide validate` on a `graylog` block (graylog-1.0); never fails a rule. The block sets no `streams` and no `stream_categories`, and a tenant it deploys to sets no default `streams`: Graylog then searches every stream the token's user can read.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    query: EventID:1\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nSet `streams` on the block, or default `streams` on the tenant's `[tenants.setup]`.\n",
                "text": "graylog_all_streams\n\nA Graylog definition names no stream, so it searches every stream.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by opentide validate on a graylog block (graylog-1.0); never fails a rule. The block sets no streams and no stream_categories, and a tenant it deploys to sets no default streams: Graylog then searches every stream the token's user can read.\n\nExample\n\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    query: EventID:1\n\nHow to fix\n\nSet streams on the block, or default streams on the tenant's [tenants.setup]."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_all_streams",
              "id": "graylog_all_streams",
              "name": "GraylogAllStreams",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog definition names no stream, so it searches every stream."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A regex, wildcard or range applies to Graylog's analysed message field."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_analyzed_field\n\nA regex, wildcard or range applies to Graylog's analysed message field.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by the analysis engine on a Graylog query (graylog-1.0, Query diagnostics): the `query` of a `graylog` block and `.lucene` files under `--platform graylog`, in `opentide validate`, `opentide validate query`, `opentide lint`, the editor and `opentide tui`. `message` and `full_message` are tokenised and lower-cased, so a regex or wildcard matches single tokens, never the whole value.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    query: message:/.*powershell -enc.*/\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nMatch a keyword field the pipeline extracts, or search the words as a phrase (`message:\"powershell -enc\"`).\n",
                "text": "graylog_analyzed_field\n\nA regex, wildcard or range applies to Graylog's analysed message field.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by the analysis engine on a Graylog query (graylog-1.0, Query diagnostics): the query of a graylog block and .lucene files under --platform graylog, in opentide validate, opentide validate query, opentide lint, the editor and opentide tui. message and full_message are tokenised and lower-cased, so a regex or wildcard matches single tokens, never the whole value.\n\nExample\n\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    query: message:/.*powershell -enc.*/\n\nHow to fix\n\nMatch a keyword field the pipeline extracts, or search the words as a phrase (message:\"powershell -enc\")."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_analyzed_field",
              "id": "graylog_analyzed_field",
              "name": "GraylogAnalyzedField",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A regex, wildcard or range applies to Graylog's analysed message field."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog create got no answer, so the definition may exist without a binding."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_create_unconfirmed\n\nA Graylog create got no answer, so the definition may exist without a binding.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide deploy` on a `graylog` block (graylog-1.0). `POST /api/events/definitions` got no answer (a timeout, a reset), or answered no id. The create may have succeeded, but its id never reached opentide, so nothing was recorded. Titles are not unique, so deploy does not retry, which could create a second definition, and never lists definitions to find it.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngraylog_create_unconfirmed: the create got no answer, so it may have succeeded with an id opentide\ndoes not know. Find the definition by the title 'mshta remote script' or the tag\n'opentide.00000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000061' in Graylog\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nFind the definition by its title (on 7.2, by the `opentide.<uuid>` tag). Delete it and deploy again, or record its 24-hex id under `platforms.graylog.<tenant>` in the rule's `.opentide/states/deployment/<uuid>.json` and commit it.\n",
                "text": "graylog_create_unconfirmed\n\nA Graylog create got no answer, so the definition may exist without a binding.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide deploy on a graylog block (graylog-1.0). POST /api/events/definitions got no answer (a timeout, a reset), or answered no id. The create may have succeeded, but its id never reached opentide, so nothing was recorded. Titles are not unique, so deploy does not retry, which could create a second definition, and never lists definitions to find it.\n\nExample\n\ngraylog_create_unconfirmed: the create got no answer, so it may have succeeded with an id opentide\ndoes not know. Find the definition by the title 'mshta remote script' or the tag\n'opentide.00000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000061' in Graylog\n\nHow to fix\n\nFind the definition by its title (on 7.2, by the opentide.<uuid> tag). Delete it and deploy again, or record its 24-hex id under platforms.graylog.<tenant> in the rule's .opentide/states/deployment/<uuid>.json and commit it."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_create_unconfirmed",
              "id": "graylog_create_unconfirmed",
              "name": "GraylogCreateUnconfirmed",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog create got no answer, so the definition may exist without a binding."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Graylog refuses to change or delete an event definition outside the default scope."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_definition_immutable\n\nGraylog refuses to change or delete an event definition outside the default scope.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide deploy` on a `graylog` block (graylog-1.0). Graylog answered \"Immutable entity cannot be modified\" or \"Non-deletable entity cannot be deleted\": the definition's `_scope` is not `DEFAULT` (a content pack or Illuminate installed it). Deploy always sends `_scope: DEFAULT` and never changes a scope.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngraylog_definition_immutable: Immutable entity cannot be modified; deploy never changes a definition's scope\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nPoint the rule's binding in `.opentide/states/deployment/<uuid>.json` at a definition opentide created, or remove it so deploy creates one.\n",
                "text": "graylog_definition_immutable\n\nGraylog refuses to change or delete an event definition outside the default scope.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide deploy on a graylog block (graylog-1.0). Graylog answered \"Immutable entity cannot be modified\" or \"Non-deletable entity cannot be deleted\": the definition's _scope is not DEFAULT (a content pack or Illuminate installed it). Deploy always sends _scope: DEFAULT and never changes a scope.\n\nExample\n\ngraylog_definition_immutable: Immutable entity cannot be modified; deploy never changes a definition's scope\n\nHow to fix\n\nPoint the rule's binding in .opentide/states/deployment/<uuid>.json at a definition opentide created, or remove it so deploy creates one."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_definition_immutable",
              "id": "graylog_definition_immutable",
              "name": "GraylogDefinitionImmutable",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Graylog refuses to change or delete an event definition outside the default scope."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Graylog refuses to delete an event definition another one depends on."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_definition_in_use\n\nGraylog refuses to delete an event definition another one depends on.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide deploy` on a `graylog` block (graylog-1.0) under `DELETION`. Another definition (a correlation) depends on this one, so Graylog keeps it, and the binding stays.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngraylog_definition_in_use: another definition depends on this one: ...\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nRemove or change the dependent definitions named in the message, then deploy again.\n",
                "text": "graylog_definition_in_use\n\nGraylog refuses to delete an event definition another one depends on.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide deploy on a graylog block (graylog-1.0) under DELETION. Another definition (a correlation) depends on this one, so Graylog keeps it, and the binding stays.\n\nExample\n\ngraylog_definition_in_use: another definition depends on this one: ...\n\nHow to fix\n\nRemove or change the dependent definitions named in the message, then deploy again."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_definition_in_use",
              "id": "graylog_definition_in_use",
              "name": "GraylogDefinitionInUse",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Graylog refuses to delete an event definition another one depends on."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Graylog refused an event definition, naming the fields at fault."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_definition_invalid\n\nGraylog refused an event definition, naming the fields at fault.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide deploy` on a `graylog` block (graylog-1.0). Graylog answered 400 with `{\"errors\": {<field>: [<message>]}}`: a value it does not accept, such as a stream or notification id that does not exist.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngraylog_definition_invalid: notifications: Notification 65f0c0ffee0123456789abcf doesn't exist\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nFix the field Graylog names: for an id, the tenant's `stream_ids` or `notification_ids` table.\n",
                "text": "graylog_definition_invalid\n\nGraylog refused an event definition, naming the fields at fault.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide deploy on a graylog block (graylog-1.0). Graylog answered 400 with {\"errors\": {<field>: [<message>]}}: a value it does not accept, such as a stream or notification id that does not exist.\n\nExample\n\ngraylog_definition_invalid: notifications: Notification 65f0c0ffee0123456789abcf doesn't exist\n\nHow to fix\n\nFix the field Graylog names: for an id, the tenant's stream_ids or notification_ids table."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_definition_invalid",
              "id": "graylog_definition_invalid",
              "name": "GraylogDefinitionInvalid",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Graylog refused an event definition, naming the fields at fault."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The recorded Graylog event definition was gone when deploy came to disable it."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_definition_missing\n\nThe recorded Graylog event definition was gone when deploy came to disable it.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by `opentide deploy` on a `graylog` block (graylog-1.0) under `DISABLEMENT`. `PUT` answered 404; there is nothing to disable, so deploy creates nothing and removes the id from `.opentide/states/deployment/`.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngraylog_definition_missing: the recorded definition no longer exists on the tenant; its id is removed\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nCommit the updated deployment state.\n",
                "text": "graylog_definition_missing\n\nThe recorded Graylog event definition was gone when deploy came to disable it.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by opentide deploy on a graylog block (graylog-1.0) under DISABLEMENT. PUT answered 404; there is nothing to disable, so deploy creates nothing and removes the id from .opentide/states/deployment/.\n\nExample\n\ngraylog_definition_missing: the recorded definition no longer exists on the tenant; its id is removed\n\nHow to fix\n\nCommit the updated deployment state."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_definition_missing",
              "id": "graylog_definition_missing",
              "name": "GraylogDefinitionMissing",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The recorded Graylog event definition was gone when deploy came to disable it."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The recorded Graylog event definition was gone, so deploy created it again."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_definition_recreated\n\nThe recorded Graylog event definition was gone, so deploy created it again.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by `opentide deploy` on a `graylog` block (graylog-1.0). `PUT /api/events/definitions/{id}` answered 404: someone deleted the definition in Graylog. Deploy creates it again and replaces the id in `.opentide/states/deployment/`.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngraylog_definition_recreated: the recorded definition no longer exists on the tenant; creating it again\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nCommit the updated deployment state. Delete definitions through a `DELETION` status, not in Graylog.\n",
                "text": "graylog_definition_recreated\n\nThe recorded Graylog event definition was gone, so deploy created it again.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by opentide deploy on a graylog block (graylog-1.0). PUT /api/events/definitions/{id} answered 404: someone deleted the definition in Graylog. Deploy creates it again and replaces the id in .opentide/states/deployment/.\n\nExample\n\ngraylog_definition_recreated: the recorded definition no longer exists on the tenant; creating it again\n\nHow to fix\n\nCommit the updated deployment state. Delete definitions through a DELETION status, not in Graylog."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_definition_recreated",
              "id": "graylog_definition_recreated",
              "name": "GraylogDefinitionRecreated",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The recorded Graylog event definition was gone, so deploy created it again."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog query holds four backslashes in a row."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_double_backslash\n\nA Graylog query holds four backslashes in a row.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by the analysis engine on a Graylog query (graylog-1.0, Query diagnostics): the `query` of a `graylog` block and `.lucene` files under `--platform graylog`, in `opentide validate`, `opentide validate query`, `opentide lint`, the editor and `opentide tui`. In a Graylog query, `\\\\` is one literal backslash, so `\\\\\\\\` matches two, which a Windows path does not have.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    query: 'image_lc:/.*\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mshta\\.exe/'\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nWrite one literal backslash as `\\\\` in the query text (in single-quoted YAML, as written).\n",
                "text": "graylog_double_backslash\n\nA Graylog query holds four backslashes in a row.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by the analysis engine on a Graylog query (graylog-1.0, Query diagnostics): the query of a graylog block and .lucene files under --platform graylog, in opentide validate, opentide validate query, opentide lint, the editor and opentide tui. In a Graylog query, \\\\ is one literal backslash, so \\\\\\\\ matches two, which a Windows path does not have.\n\nExample\n\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    query: 'image_lc:/.*\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\mshta\\.exe/'\n\nHow to fix\n\nWrite one literal backslash as \\\\ in the query text (in single-quoted YAML, as written)."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_double_backslash",
              "id": "graylog_double_backslash",
              "name": "GraylogDoubleBackslash",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog query holds four backslashes in a row."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog event field has an invalid name or value."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_event_field\n\nA Graylog event field has an invalid name or value.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide validate` on a `graylog` block (graylog-1.0), at `event_fields.<name>`. A name matches `[A-Za-z0-9_.@-]+`. A value is a bare message field name (copied from the message that fired the event), a string containing `${` (a Graylog template), `{template: ...}`, or `{lookup: {table, key_field}}`.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    event_fields:\n      family: two words\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nWrite constant text as `{template: \"two words\"}`, or name a message field.\n",
                "text": "graylog_event_field\n\nA Graylog event field has an invalid name or value.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide validate on a graylog block (graylog-1.0), at event_fields.<name>. A name matches [A-Za-z0-9_.@-]+. A value is a bare message field name (copied from the message that fired the event), a string containing ${ (a Graylog template), {template: ...}, or {lookup: {table, key_field}}.\n\nExample\n\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    event_fields:\n      family: two words\n\nHow to fix\n\nWrite constant text as {template: \"two words\"}, or name a message field."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_event_field",
              "id": "graylog_event_field",
              "name": "GraylogEventField",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog event field has an invalid name or value."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog event_key item is not an event field of the block."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_event_key\n\nA Graylog event_key item is not an event field of the block.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide validate` on a `graylog` block (graylog-1.0), at `event_key`. Graylog builds the event key from event fields, and refuses a key field `event_fields` does not define.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    event_fields: {host: source}\n    event_key: user\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nAdd the field to `event_fields`, or key on one that is there.\n",
                "text": "graylog_event_key\n\nA Graylog event_key item is not an event field of the block.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide validate on a graylog block (graylog-1.0), at event_key. Graylog builds the event key from event fields, and refuses a key field event_fields does not define.\n\nExample\n\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    event_fields: {host: source}\n    event_key: user\n\nHow to fix\n\nAdd the field to event_fields, or key on one that is there."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_event_key",
              "id": "graylog_event_key",
              "name": "GraylogEventKey",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog event_key item is not an event field of the block."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog block's event_limit is out of range, or set on an aggregation."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_event_limit\n\nA Graylog block's event_limit is out of range, or set on an aggregation.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide validate` on a `graylog` block (graylog-1.0), at `event_limit`. `event_limit` caps the events one run of a filter creates: an integer from 1 to 1,000. An aggregation creates one event per group and takes none.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    event_limit: 5000\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nSet a value from 1 to 1,000, or remove `event_limit` from an aggregation.\n",
                "text": "graylog_event_limit\n\nA Graylog block's event_limit is out of range, or set on an aggregation.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide validate on a graylog block (graylog-1.0), at event_limit. event_limit caps the events one run of a filter creates: an integer from 1 to 1,000. An aggregation creates one event per group and takes none.\n\nExample\n\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    event_limit: 5000\n\nHow to fix\n\nSet a value from 1 to 1,000, or remove event_limit from an aggregation."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_event_limit",
              "id": "graylog_event_limit",
              "name": "GraylogEventLimit",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog block's event_limit is out of range, or set on an aggregation."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog aggregation groups by a name that is not a field name."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_field_name\n\nA Graylog aggregation groups by a name that is not a field name.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide validate` on a `graylog` block (graylog-1.0), at `aggregation.group_by`. A Graylog field name matches `[A-Za-z0-9_.@-]+`.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    aggregation:\n      group_by: [src ip]\n      condition: count() > 1\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nGroup by the field's real name (`src_ip`).\n",
                "text": "graylog_field_name\n\nA Graylog aggregation groups by a name that is not a field name.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide validate on a graylog block (graylog-1.0), at aggregation.group_by. A Graylog field name matches [A-Za-z0-9_.@-]+.\n\nExample\n\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    aggregation:\n      group_by: [src ip]\n      condition: count() > 1\n\nHow to fix\n\nGroup by the field's real name (src_ip)."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_field_name",
              "id": "graylog_field_name",
              "name": "GraylogFieldName",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog aggregation groups by a name that is not a field name."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog block sets a field the tenant's version cannot take."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_field_unsupported_version\n\nA Graylog block sets a field the tenant's version cannot take.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by `opentide deploy` on a `graylog` block (graylog-1.0). `summary` (the event summary template) needs Graylog 7.1, and block `tags` need 7.2. Deploy leaves the field out of the request rather than let Graylog 6.3 drop it silently or 7.x refuse it.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngraylog_field_unsupported_version: summary is left out: Graylog 6.3.5 has no event summary template (7.1 and later)\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nUpgrade the tenant, or remove the field from the block.\n",
                "text": "graylog_field_unsupported_version\n\nA Graylog block sets a field the tenant's version cannot take.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by opentide deploy on a graylog block (graylog-1.0). summary (the event summary template) needs Graylog 7.1, and block tags need 7.2. Deploy leaves the field out of the request rather than let Graylog 6.3 drop it silently or 7.x refuse it.\n\nExample\n\ngraylog_field_unsupported_version: summary is left out: Graylog 6.3.5 has no event summary template (7.1 and later)\n\nHow to fix\n\nUpgrade the tenant, or remove the field from the block."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_field_unsupported_version",
              "id": "graylog_field_unsupported_version",
              "name": "GraylogFieldUnsupportedVersion",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog block sets a field the tenant's version cannot take."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Graylog refused a request for want of a permission."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_forbidden\n\nGraylog refused a request for want of a permission.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide deploy` on a `graylog` block (graylog-1.0). Graylog answered 403; the rule fails and the next one is sent. The token's user needs `eventdefinitions:create`, `eventdefinitions:read`, `eventdefinitions:edit` and `eventdefinitions:delete`, and `streams:read` on every stream a definition names.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngraylog_forbidden: POST /api/events/definitions?schedule=true was refused (HTTP 403)\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nGrant the token's user the permissions, most often `streams:read` on the definition's streams.\n",
                "text": "graylog_forbidden\n\nGraylog refused a request for want of a permission.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide deploy on a graylog block (graylog-1.0). Graylog answered 403; the rule fails and the next one is sent. The token's user needs eventdefinitions:create, eventdefinitions:read, eventdefinitions:edit and eventdefinitions:delete, and streams:read on every stream a definition names.\n\nExample\n\ngraylog_forbidden: POST /api/events/definitions?schedule=true was refused (HTTP 403)\n\nHow to fix\n\nGrant the token's user the permissions, most often streams:read on the definition's streams."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_forbidden",
              "id": "graylog_forbidden",
              "name": "GraylogForbidden",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Graylog refused a request for want of a permission."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog query term starts with `*` or `?`."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_leading_wildcard\n\nA Graylog query term starts with `*` or `?`.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by the analysis engine on a Graylog query (graylog-1.0, Query diagnostics): the `query` of a `graylog` block and `.lucene` files under `--platform graylog`, in `opentide validate`, `opentide validate query`, `opentide lint`, the editor and `opentide tui`. Graylog passes the query to `query_string` with `allow_leading_wildcard` from the server's `allow_leading_wildcard_searches`, `false` by default, and refuses the query (\"'*' or '?' not allowed as first character\"). opentide drops the finding when every tenant the block deploys to sets `allow_leading_wildcard = true`; offline, with no tenant, it is an error.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    query: image_lc:*\\\\mshta.exe\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nWrite a regex with a leading `.*` (`image_lc:/.*\\\\mshta\\.exe/`), or set `allow_leading_wildcard = true` on every tenant whose server allows leading wildcards.\n",
                "text": "graylog_leading_wildcard\n\nA Graylog query term starts with * or ?.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by the analysis engine on a Graylog query (graylog-1.0, Query diagnostics): the query of a graylog block and .lucene files under --platform graylog, in opentide validate, opentide validate query, opentide lint, the editor and opentide tui. Graylog passes the query to query_string with allow_leading_wildcard from the server's allow_leading_wildcard_searches, false by default, and refuses the query (\"'*' or '?' not allowed as first character\"). opentide drops the finding when every tenant the block deploys to sets allow_leading_wildcard = true; offline, with no tenant, it is an error.\n\nExample\n\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    query: image_lc:*\\\\mshta.exe\n\nHow to fix\n\nWrite a regex with a leading .* (image_lc:/.*\\\\mshta\\.exe/), or set allow_leading_wildcard = true on every tenant whose server allows leading wildcards."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_leading_wildcard",
              "id": "graylog_leading_wildcard",
              "name": "GraylogLeadingWildcard",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog query term starts with `*` or `?`."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog list field is not a string or a list of distinct, non-blank strings."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_list_shape\n\nA Graylog list field is not a string or a list of distinct, non-blank strings.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide validate` on a `graylog` block (graylog-1.0), at the list field: `tags`, `streams`, `stream_categories`, `event_key`, `notifications` or `aggregation.group_by`. Each takes one string or a list of strings; a string is a one-item list.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    streams: [sysmon, sysmon]\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nWrite one string, or a list whose items are non-blank and not repeated.\n",
                "text": "graylog_list_shape\n\nA Graylog list field is not a string or a list of distinct, non-blank strings.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide validate on a graylog block (graylog-1.0), at the list field: tags, streams, stream_categories, event_key, notifications or aggregation.group_by. Each takes one string or a list of strings; a string is a one-item list.\n\nExample\n\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    streams: [sysmon, sysmon]\n\nHow to fix\n\nWrite one string, or a list whose items are non-blank and not repeated."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_list_shape",
              "id": "graylog_list_shape",
              "name": "GraylogListShape",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog list field is not a string or a list of distinct, non-blank strings."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog range has an open end, which Graylog's validator refuses."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_open_range\n\nA Graylog range has an open end, which Graylog's validator refuses.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by the analysis engine on a Graylog query (graylog-1.0, Query diagnostics): the `query` of a `graylog` block and `.lucene` files under `--platform graylog`, in `opentide validate`, `opentide validate query`, `opentide lint`, the editor and `opentide tui`. `[1 TO *]` and `{* TO 5}` are refused by `POST /api/search/validate`, so deploy would never save the definition.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    query: EventID:[4624 TO *]\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nWrite the bound as a comparison (`EventID:>=4624`), or give both ends.\n",
                "text": "graylog_open_range\n\nA Graylog range has an open end, which Graylog's validator refuses.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by the analysis engine on a Graylog query (graylog-1.0, Query diagnostics): the query of a graylog block and .lucene files under --platform graylog, in opentide validate, opentide validate query, opentide lint, the editor and opentide tui. [1 TO *] and {* TO 5} are refused by POST /api/search/validate, so deploy would never save the definition.\n\nExample\n\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    query: EventID:[4624 TO *]\n\nHow to fix\n\nWrite the bound as a comparison (EventID:>=4624), or give both ends."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_open_range",
              "id": "graylog_open_range",
              "name": "GraylogOpenRange",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog range has an open end, which Graylog's validator refuses."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog filter searches a window longer than its interval."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_overlapping_window\n\nA Graylog filter searches a window longer than its interval.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by `opentide validate` on a `graylog` block (graylog-1.0), at `schedule`; never fails a rule. With `window` longer than `every`, consecutive runs search overlapping time ranges, and a filter (no `aggregation`) creates an event for each message once per run that sees it.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    schedule: {every: 5m, window: 15m}\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nMake `window` equal to `every` (the default), or turn the filter into an aggregation.\n",
                "text": "graylog_overlapping_window\n\nA Graylog filter searches a window longer than its interval.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by opentide validate on a graylog block (graylog-1.0), at schedule; never fails a rule. With window longer than every, consecutive runs search overlapping time ranges, and a filter (no aggregation) creates an event for each message once per run that sees it.\n\nExample\n\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    schedule: {every: 5m, window: 15m}\n\nHow to fix\n\nMake window equal to every (the default), or turn the filter into an aggregation."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_overlapping_window",
              "id": "graylog_overlapping_window",
              "name": "GraylogOverlappingWindow",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog filter searches a window longer than its interval."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog block's priority is not one of Graylog's priorities."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_priority\n\nA Graylog block's priority is not one of Graylog's priorities.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide validate` on a `graylog` block (graylog-1.0), at `priority`. The priorities are `Informational`, `Low`, `Medium`, `High` and `Critical`.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    priority: Urgent\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nWrite one of the five, or leave `priority` out to derive it from `response.alert_severity`.\n",
                "text": "graylog_priority\n\nA Graylog block's priority is not one of Graylog's priorities.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide validate on a graylog block (graylog-1.0), at priority. The priorities are Informational, Low, Medium, High and Critical.\n\nExample\n\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    priority: Urgent\n\nHow to fix\n\nWrite one of the five, or leave priority out to derive it from response.alert_severity."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_priority",
              "id": "graylog_priority",
              "name": "GraylogPriority",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog block's priority is not one of Graylog's priorities."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Graylog before 7.1 has no Informational priority: the definition is sent as Low."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_priority_collapsed\n\nGraylog before 7.1 has no Informational priority: the definition is sent as Low.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by `opentide deploy` on a `graylog` block (graylog-1.0). The priority comes from the block's `priority`, else `response.alert_severity`. Graylog 7.1 added priority 0 (Informational); before it, deploy sends 1 (Low).\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngraylog_priority_collapsed: Graylog 7.0.16 has no Informational priority: sent as Low (1)\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nUpgrade the tenant to 7.1 or later, or set `priority: Low` on the block.\n",
                "text": "graylog_priority_collapsed\n\nGraylog before 7.1 has no Informational priority: the definition is sent as Low.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by opentide deploy on a graylog block (graylog-1.0). The priority comes from the block's priority, else response.alert_severity. Graylog 7.1 added priority 0 (Informational); before it, deploy sends 1 (Low).\n\nExample\n\ngraylog_priority_collapsed: Graylog 7.0.16 has no Informational priority: sent as Low (1)\n\nHow to fix\n\nUpgrade the tenant to 7.1 or later, or set priority: Low on the block."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_priority_collapsed",
              "id": "graylog_priority_collapsed",
              "name": "GraylogPriorityCollapsed",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Graylog before 7.1 has no Informational priority: the definition is sent as Low."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog block has no query, or an empty one."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_query_blank\n\nA Graylog block has no query, or an empty one.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide validate` on a `graylog` block (graylog-1.0), at `query`. Every event definition searches with a query; an empty one would search every message.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    status: STAGING\n    query: \"\"\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nWrite the Graylog search query, for example `query: EventID:1 AND image_lc:/.*mshta\\.exe/`.\n",
                "text": "graylog_query_blank\n\nA Graylog block has no query, or an empty one.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide validate on a graylog block (graylog-1.0), at query. Every event definition searches with a query; an empty one would search every message.\n\nExample\n\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    status: STAGING\n    query: \"\"\n\nHow to fix\n\nWrite the Graylog search query, for example query: EventID:1 AND image_lc:/.*mshta\\.exe/."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_query_blank",
              "id": "graylog_query_blank",
              "name": "GraylogQueryBlank",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog block has no query, or an empty one."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Graylog refused a query, so deploy saved nothing."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_query_invalid\n\nGraylog refused a query, so deploy saved nothing.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide deploy` on a `graylog` block (graylog-1.0). Before every create and every update but a `DISABLEMENT`, deploy sends the query to `POST /api/search/validate`; Graylog stores a query it cannot parse without complaint, so an `ERROR` verdict fails the rule and no request saves it.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngraylog_query_invalid: Graylog refuses the query, so nothing is saved: Query parsing error:\nCannot parse query, cause: incomplete query, query ended unexpectedly (line 1, column 0)\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nFix the query at the line and column Graylog names, and check it with `opentide validate query --platform graylog --live`.\n",
                "text": "graylog_query_invalid\n\nGraylog refused a query, so deploy saved nothing.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide deploy on a graylog block (graylog-1.0). Before every create and every update but a DISABLEMENT, deploy sends the query to POST /api/search/validate; Graylog stores a query it cannot parse without complaint, so an ERROR verdict fails the rule and no request saves it.\n\nExample\n\ngraylog_query_invalid: Graylog refuses the query, so nothing is saved: Query parsing error:\nCannot parse query, cause: incomplete query, query ended unexpectedly (line 1, column 0)\n\nHow to fix\n\nFix the query at the line and column Graylog names, and check it with opentide validate query --platform graylog --live."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_query_invalid",
              "id": "graylog_query_invalid",
              "name": "GraylogQueryInvalid",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Graylog refused a query, so deploy saved nothing."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Graylog has a reservation about a query."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_query_warning\n\nGraylog has a reservation about a query.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by `opentide deploy` on a `graylog` block (graylog-1.0) and `opentide validate query --live`: a `WARNING` or `INFO` explanation of `POST /api/search/validate` that is neither an unknown field nor a value type, such as `INVALID_OPERATOR`.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngraylog_query_warning: Invalid operator: Query contains invalid operator \"and\" (line 1, column 10)\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nRead Graylog's explanation and change the query; an operator is upper case (`AND`).\n",
                "text": "graylog_query_warning\n\nGraylog has a reservation about a query.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by opentide deploy on a graylog block (graylog-1.0) and opentide validate query --live: a WARNING or INFO explanation of POST /api/search/validate that is neither an unknown field nor a value type, such as INVALID_OPERATOR.\n\nExample\n\ngraylog_query_warning: Invalid operator: Query contains invalid operator \"and\" (line 1, column 10)\n\nHow to fix\n\nRead Graylog's explanation and change the query; an operator is upper case (AND)."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_query_warning",
              "id": "graylog_query_warning",
              "name": "GraylogQueryWarning",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Graylog has a reservation about a query."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog regex holds a character Lucene reads as a regex operator."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_regex_operator\n\nA Graylog regex holds a character Lucene reads as a regex operator.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by the analysis engine on a Graylog query (graylog-1.0, Query diagnostics): the `query` of a `graylog` block and `.lucene` files under `--platform graylog`, in `opentide validate`, `opentide validate query`, `opentide lint`, the editor and `opentide tui`. An unescaped `#`, `&`, `@`, `<`, `>` or `~`, other than a numeric interval `<n-m>`, is an operator in Lucene's regex syntax, and Graylog's validator still says OK. `@` is \"any string\": `/.*@contoso\\.com/` also matches `alicecontoso.com`; write `\\@`.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    query: url_lc:/.*#section.*/\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nEscape the character (`\\#`) when you mean it literally.\n",
                "text": "graylog_regex_operator\n\nA Graylog regex holds a character Lucene reads as a regex operator.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by the analysis engine on a Graylog query (graylog-1.0, Query diagnostics): the query of a graylog block and .lucene files under --platform graylog, in opentide validate, opentide validate query, opentide lint, the editor and opentide tui. An unescaped #, &, @, <, > or ~, other than a numeric interval <n-m>, is an operator in Lucene's regex syntax, and Graylog's validator still says OK. @ is \"any string\": /.*@contoso\\.com/ also matches alicecontoso.com; write \\@.\n\nExample\n\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    query: url_lc:/.*#section.*/\n\nHow to fix\n\nEscape the character (\\#) when you mean it literally."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_regex_operator",
              "id": "graylog_regex_operator",
              "name": "GraylogRegexOperator",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog regex holds a character Lucene reads as a regex operator."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog regex holds an unescaped double quote."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_regex_quote\n\nA Graylog regex holds an unescaped double quote.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by the analysis engine on a Graylog query (graylog-1.0, Query diagnostics): the `query` of a `graylog` block and `.lucene` files under `--platform graylog`, in `opentide validate`, `opentide validate query`, `opentide lint`, the editor and `opentide tui`. The regex then matches nothing.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    query: 'command_line_lc:/.*\"c:.*/'\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nEscape the quote (`\\\"`), or match without it.\n",
                "text": "graylog_regex_quote\n\nA Graylog regex holds an unescaped double quote.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by the analysis engine on a Graylog query (graylog-1.0, Query diagnostics): the query of a graylog block and .lucene files under --platform graylog, in opentide validate, opentide validate query, opentide lint, the editor and opentide tui. The regex then matches nothing.\n\nExample\n\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    query: 'command_line_lc:/.*\"c:.*/'\n\nHow to fix\n\nEscape the quote (\\\"), or match without it."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_regex_quote",
              "id": "graylog_regex_quote",
              "name": "GraylogRegexQuote",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog regex holds an unescaped double quote."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog regex uses syntax Lucene's regex engine does not have."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_regex_unsupported\n\nA Graylog regex uses syntax Lucene's regex engine does not have.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by the analysis engine on a Graylog query (graylog-1.0, Query diagnostics): the `query` of a `graylog` block and `.lucene` files under `--platform graylog`, in `opentide validate`, `opentide validate query`, `opentide lint`, the editor and `opentide tui`. An inline flag such as `(?i)`, `\\b`, `\\B`, lookahead or lookbehind, or a backreference: Lucene's regex engine accepts none of them, and Graylog matches nothing without saying so.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    query: command_line:/(?i).*mimikatz.*/\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nMatch a lower-cased copy of the field instead (`command_line_lc:/.*mimikatz.*/`), written by a tenant pipeline; see the case section of the Graylog platform spec.\n",
                "text": "graylog_regex_unsupported\n\nA Graylog regex uses syntax Lucene's regex engine does not have.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by the analysis engine on a Graylog query (graylog-1.0, Query diagnostics): the query of a graylog block and .lucene files under --platform graylog, in opentide validate, opentide validate query, opentide lint, the editor and opentide tui. An inline flag such as (?i), \\b, \\B, lookahead or lookbehind, or a backreference: Lucene's regex engine accepts none of them, and Graylog matches nothing without saying so.\n\nExample\n\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    query: command_line:/(?i).*mimikatz.*/\n\nHow to fix\n\nMatch a lower-cased copy of the field instead (command_line_lc:/.*mimikatz.*/), written by a tenant pipeline; see the case section of the Graylog platform spec."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_regex_unsupported",
              "id": "graylog_regex_unsupported",
              "name": "GraylogRegexUnsupported",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog regex uses syntax Lucene's regex engine does not have."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog request failed for a reason the failure table does not name."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_request_failed\n\nA Graylog request failed for a reason the failure table does not name.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide deploy` on a `graylog` block (graylog-1.0): an answer that is not 2xx and none of 400 with field errors, 401, 403, 404 on update, an immutable entity or a definition in use, such as a 500.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngraylog_request_failed: PUT /api/events/definitions/65f1c0de2a3b4c5d6e7f8091?schedule=true: HTTP 500: ...\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nRead Graylog's message and server log, then deploy again.\n",
                "text": "graylog_request_failed\n\nA Graylog request failed for a reason the failure table does not name.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide deploy on a graylog block (graylog-1.0): an answer that is not 2xx and none of 400 with field errors, 401, 403, 404 on update, an immutable entity or a definition in use, such as a 500.\n\nExample\n\ngraylog_request_failed: PUT /api/events/definitions/65f1c0de2a3b4c5d6e7f8091?schedule=true: HTTP 500: ...\n\nHow to fix\n\nRead Graylog's message and server log, then deploy again."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_request_failed",
              "id": "graylog_request_failed",
              "name": "GraylogRequestFailed",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog request failed for a reason the failure table does not name."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog block's schedule is invalid."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_schedule\n\nA Graylog block's schedule is invalid.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide validate` on a `graylog` block (graylog-1.0), at `schedule` or the offending `schedule.<key>`. Exactly one of `every` and `cron` is set (or neither); `every` and `window` are durations greater than zero (`5m`, `1h10m`, `PT15M`); `cron` is a Quartz expression of 6 or 7 fields with `?` in day of month or day of week; `timezone` is an IANA zone name; `window` is set when `cron` is.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    schedule: {cron: \"0 */5 * * * ?\"}\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nFix the key the finding names: with `cron`, also set `window` (`schedule: {cron: \"0 */5 * * * ?\", window: 5m}`).\n",
                "text": "graylog_schedule\n\nA Graylog block's schedule is invalid.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide validate on a graylog block (graylog-1.0), at schedule or the offending schedule.<key>. Exactly one of every and cron is set (or neither); every and window are durations greater than zero (5m, 1h10m, PT15M); cron is a Quartz expression of 6 or 7 fields with ? in day of month or day of week; timezone is an IANA zone name; window is set when cron is.\n\nExample\n\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    schedule: {cron: \"0 */5 * * * ?\"}\n\nHow to fix\n\nFix the key the finding names: with cron, also set window (schedule: {cron: \"0 */5 * * * ?\", window: 5m})."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_schedule",
              "id": "graylog_schedule",
              "name": "GraylogSchedule",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog block's schedule is invalid."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog tenant refused the access token."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_tenant_unauthorized\n\nA Graylog tenant refused the access token.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide deploy` on a `graylog` tenant (graylog-1.0). Graylog answered 401; no rule on the tenant deploys. The token is sent as HTTP Basic, the token as user name and `token` as password.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngraylog_tenant_unauthorized: tenant graylog-prod refused the access token: HTTP 401\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nSet the token's environment variable (`token = \"$GRAYLOG_TOKEN\"`) to a valid access token; access tokens expire after 30 days by default.\n",
                "text": "graylog_tenant_unauthorized\n\nA Graylog tenant refused the access token.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide deploy on a graylog tenant (graylog-1.0). Graylog answered 401; no rule on the tenant deploys. The token is sent as HTTP Basic, the token as user name and token as password.\n\nExample\n\ngraylog_tenant_unauthorized: tenant graylog-prod refused the access token: HTTP 401\n\nHow to fix\n\nSet the token's environment variable (token = \"$GRAYLOG_TOKEN\") to a valid access token; access tokens expire after 30 days by default."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_tenant_unauthorized",
              "id": "graylog_tenant_unauthorized",
              "name": "GraylogTenantUnauthorized",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog tenant refused the access token."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog tenant did not answer."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_tenant_unreachable\n\nA Graylog tenant did not answer.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide deploy` on a `graylog` tenant (graylog-1.0): DNS, a refused or reset connection, TLS or a timeout on a request other than a create. No further rule on the tenant deploys.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngraylog_tenant_unreachable: Cannot reach graylog tenant 'lab' at https://graylog.example.com: connection refused\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nCheck the tenant's `url`, the network path, and `ssl`.\n",
                "text": "graylog_tenant_unreachable\n\nA Graylog tenant did not answer.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide deploy on a graylog tenant (graylog-1.0): DNS, a refused or reset connection, TLS or a timeout on a request other than a create. No further rule on the tenant deploys.\n\nExample\n\ngraylog_tenant_unreachable: Cannot reach graylog tenant 'lab' at https://graylog.example.com: connection refused\n\nHow to fix\n\nCheck the tenant's url, the network path, and ssl."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_tenant_unreachable",
              "id": "graylog_tenant_unreachable",
              "name": "GraylogTenantUnreachable",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog tenant did not answer."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Two enabled Graylog blocks resolve to one event definition title."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_title_duplicate\n\nTwo enabled Graylog blocks resolve to one event definition title.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by `opentide validate` on a `graylog` block (graylog-1.0); never fails a rule. The title is the block's `name`, else the rule's `name`. Graylog allows two definitions with one title, and its alert list cannot tell them apart.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\n# rule-a.yaml and rule-b.yaml, both:\nname: Office application spawns PowerShell\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    enabled: true\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nGive one of the blocks its own `configurations.graylog.name`, or rename one of the rules.\n",
                "text": "graylog_title_duplicate\n\nTwo enabled Graylog blocks resolve to one event definition title.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by opentide validate on a graylog block (graylog-1.0); never fails a rule. The title is the block's name, else the rule's name. Graylog allows two definitions with one title, and its alert list cannot tell them apart.\n\nExample\n\nrule-a.yaml and rule-b.yaml, both:\nname: Office application spawns PowerShell\nconfigurations:\n  graylog:\n    enabled: true\n\nHow to fix\n\nGive one of the blocks its own configurations.graylog.name, or rename one of the rules."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_title_duplicate",
              "id": "graylog_title_duplicate",
              "name": "GraylogTitleDuplicate",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Two enabled Graylog blocks resolve to one event definition title."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Graylog does not know a field the query names, on the streams it searches."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_unknown_field\n\nGraylog does not know a field the query names, on the streams it searches.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by `opentide deploy` on a `graylog` block (graylog-1.0) and `opentide validate query --live`. Graylog's `UNKNOWN_FIELD`: no index of the definition's streams maps the field, so the query matches nothing until messages carry it. Field names are case-sensitive.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngraylog_unknown_field: Unknown field: Query contains unknown field: command_line_lc (line 1, column 12)\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nCheck the field's spelling and case, the streams the definition searches, and the pipeline that writes the field.\n",
                "text": "graylog_unknown_field\n\nGraylog does not know a field the query names, on the streams it searches.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by opentide deploy on a graylog block (graylog-1.0) and opentide validate query --live. Graylog's UNKNOWN_FIELD: no index of the definition's streams maps the field, so the query matches nothing until messages carry it. Field names are case-sensitive.\n\nExample\n\ngraylog_unknown_field: Unknown field: Query contains unknown field: command_line_lc (line 1, column 12)\n\nHow to fix\n\nCheck the field's spelling and case, the streams the definition searches, and the pipeline that writes the field."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_unknown_field",
              "id": "graylog_unknown_field",
              "name": "GraylogUnknownField",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Graylog does not know a field the query names, on the streams it searches."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A query compares a Graylog field with a value of the wrong type."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_value_type\n\nA query compares a Graylog field with a value of the wrong type.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by `opentide deploy` on a `graylog` block (graylog-1.0) and `opentide validate query --live`. Graylog's `INVALID_VALUE_TYPE`: the field is mapped as a number or a date, and the value is not one.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngraylog_value_type: Invalid value type: Type of EventID is long, cannot use value abc (line 1, column 30)\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nCompare the field with a value of its type.\n",
                "text": "graylog_value_type\n\nA query compares a Graylog field with a value of the wrong type.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by opentide deploy on a graylog block (graylog-1.0) and opentide validate query --live. Graylog's INVALID_VALUE_TYPE: the field is mapped as a number or a date, and the value is not one.\n\nExample\n\ngraylog_value_type: Invalid value type: Type of EventID is long, cannot use value abc (line 1, column 30)\n\nHow to fix\n\nCompare the field with a value of its type."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_value_type",
              "id": "graylog_value_type",
              "name": "GraylogValueType",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A query compares a Graylog field with a value of the wrong type."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog tenant runs a version opentide does not deploy to."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_version_unsupported\n\nA Graylog tenant runs a version opentide does not deploy to.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide deploy` on a `graylog` tenant (graylog-1.0). Deploy reads `GET /api/system` once per tenant, before any write, and builds every request for that version. Graylog below 6.3, or a server that reports no version, stops the tenant.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngraylog_version_unsupported: tenant graylog-prod runs Graylog '6.2.4+1a2b3c4'; opentide deploys to Graylog 6.3 and later\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nUpgrade the server to Graylog 6.3 or later, or remove the tenant from `platforms/graylog.toml`.\n",
                "text": "graylog_version_unsupported\n\nA Graylog tenant runs a version opentide does not deploy to.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide deploy on a graylog tenant (graylog-1.0). Deploy reads GET /api/system once per tenant, before any write, and builds every request for that version. Graylog below 6.3, or a server that reports no version, stops the tenant.\n\nExample\n\ngraylog_version_unsupported: tenant graylog-prod runs Graylog '6.2.4+1a2b3c4'; opentide deploys to Graylog 6.3 and later\n\nHow to fix\n\nUpgrade the server to Graylog 6.3 or later, or remove the tenant from platforms/graylog.toml."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_version_unsupported",
              "id": "graylog_version_unsupported",
              "name": "GraylogVersionUnsupported",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog tenant runs a version opentide does not deploy to."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog tenant runs a version affected by the access-token revocation flaw."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# graylog_version_vulnerable\n\nA Graylog tenant runs a version affected by the access-token revocation flaw.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by `opentide deploy` on a `graylog` tenant (graylog-1.0), once per run. Graylog below 6.3.12, 7.0.16 or 7.1.12 is affected by GHSA-j769-9gv9-65gr: a revoked access token may keep working. Deploy goes on.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\ngraylog_version_vulnerable: Graylog 6.3.5 does not revoke access tokens reliably (GHSA-j769-9gv9-65gr)\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nUpgrade the server to 6.3.12, 7.0.16, 7.1.12 or later.\n",
                "text": "graylog_version_vulnerable\n\nA Graylog tenant runs a version affected by the access-token revocation flaw.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by opentide deploy on a graylog tenant (graylog-1.0), once per run. Graylog below 6.3.12, 7.0.16 or 7.1.12 is affected by GHSA-j769-9gv9-65gr: a revoked access token may keep working. Deploy goes on.\n\nExample\n\ngraylog_version_vulnerable: Graylog 6.3.5 does not revoke access tokens reliably (GHSA-j769-9gv9-65gr)\n\nHow to fix\n\nUpgrade the server to 6.3.12, 7.0.16, 7.1.12 or later."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#graylog_version_vulnerable",
              "id": "graylog_version_vulnerable",
              "name": "GraylogVersionVulnerable",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A Graylog tenant runs a version affected by the access-token revocation flaw."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A HarfangLab block sets both sigma and yara; a block is one kind."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# harfanglab_rule_kind\n\nA HarfangLab block sets both sigma and yara; a block is one kind.\n\nReported by the analysis engine for an object file: an editor running `opentide lsp start` shows it. `opentide validate` and `opentide lint` are the authority for objects and name the same problem with their own code.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nopentide validate\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nChange the file where the frame points, then run `opentide validate` again.\n",
                "text": "harfanglab_rule_kind\n\nA HarfangLab block sets both sigma and yara; a block is one kind.\n\nReported by the analysis engine for an object file: an editor running opentide lsp start shows it. opentide validate and opentide lint are the authority for objects and name the same problem with their own code.\n\nExample\n\nopentide validate\n\nHow to fix\n\nChange the file where the frame points, then run opentide validate again."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#harfanglab_rule_kind",
              "id": "harfanglab_rule_kind",
              "name": "HarfanglabRuleKind",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A HarfangLab block sets both sigma and yara; a block is one kind."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A HarfangLab YARA rule's meta `context` is not a comma-joined list of the scan contexts HarfangLab uses."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# harfanglab_yara_context\n\nA HarfangLab YARA rule's meta `context` is not a comma-joined list of the scan contexts HarfangLab uses.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by `opentide lint` (its `queries` check) on a HarfangLab rule (`harfanglab`). In the signature form, a rule that is not `private` sets meta `context` to something other than a comma-joined list, without blanks, of `file`, `memory`, `file.pe`, `file.elf`, and `file.macho` (`file,memory` and `file.pe,memory` are lists), or to a value that is not a string (`context = 1`). In the decomposed form, the `context` deploy writes from `meta.context` and `meta.os` is checked the same way. `opentide validate` does not fail on it. `file`, `memory`, and `file,memory` are the values HarfangLab's published rules use; `file.pe`, `file.elf`, and `file.macho` are what the decomposed form writes for `file`. No HarfangLab document defines the key, so another value may be accepted and never scanned.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nconfigurations:\n  harfanglab:\n    yara:\n      signature: |\n        rule Cmd_String {\n          meta:\n            context = \"disk\"   # not a HarfangLab context\n          strings:\n            $a = \"cmd.exe\" ascii\n          condition:\n            $a\n        }\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nSet `context` to where the agent should scan: `file`, `memory`, or both (`file,memory`), or a file type (`file.pe`, `file.elf`, `file.macho`), joined with commas and no spaces. In the decomposed form, list them under `meta.context`. Then run `opentide lint --check queries` again.\n\n```yaml\n          meta:\n            context = \"file,memory\"\n```\n",
                "text": "harfanglab_yara_context\n\nA HarfangLab YARA rule's meta context is not a comma-joined list of the scan contexts HarfangLab uses.\n\nSeverity: warning. Reported by opentide lint (its queries check) on a HarfangLab rule (harfanglab). In the signature form, a rule that is not private sets meta context to something other than a comma-joined list, without blanks, of file, memory, file.pe, file.elf, and file.macho (file,memory and file.pe,memory are lists), or to a value that is not a string (context = 1). In the decomposed form, the context deploy writes from meta.context and meta.os is checked the same way. opentide validate does not fail on it. file, memory, and file,memory are the values HarfangLab's published rules use; file.pe, file.elf, and file.macho are what the decomposed form writes for file. No HarfangLab document defines the key, so another value may be accepted and never scanned.\n\nExample\n\nconfigurations:\n  harfanglab:\n    yara:\n      signature: |\n        rule Cmd_String {\n          meta:\n            context = \"disk\"   # not a HarfangLab context\n          strings:\n            $a = \"cmd.exe\" ascii\n          condition:\n            $a\n        }\n\nHow to fix\n\nSet context to where the agent should scan: file, memory, or both (file,memory), or a file type (file.pe, file.elf, file.macho), joined with commas and no spaces. In the decomposed form, list them under meta.context. Then run opentide lint --check queries again.\n\n          meta:\n            context = \"file,memory\""
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#harfanglab_yara_context",
              "id": "harfanglab_yara_context",
              "name": "HarfanglabYaraContext",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A HarfangLab YARA rule's meta `context` is not a comma-joined list of the scan contexts HarfangLab uses."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A HarfangLab yara object sets signature together with decomposed keys (meta, strings, condition or imports)."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# harfanglab_yara_form\n\nA HarfangLab yara object sets signature together with decomposed keys (meta, strings, condition or imports).\n\nReported by the analysis engine for an object file: an editor running `opentide lsp start` shows it. `opentide validate` and `opentide lint` are the authority for objects and name the same problem with their own code.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nopentide validate\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nChange the file where the frame points, then run `opentide validate` again.\n",
                "text": "harfanglab_yara_form\n\nA HarfangLab yara object sets signature together with decomposed keys (meta, strings, condition or imports).\n\nReported by the analysis engine for an object file: an editor running opentide lsp start shows it. opentide validate and opentide lint are the authority for objects and name the same problem with their own code.\n\nExample\n\nopentide validate\n\nHow to fix\n\nChange the file where the frame points, then run opentide validate again."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#harfanglab_yara_form",
              "id": "harfanglab_yara_form",
              "name": "HarfanglabYaraForm",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A HarfangLab yara object sets signature together with decomposed keys (meta, strings, condition or imports)."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A decomposed HarfangLab YARA imports entry is not a module the YARA language knows."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# harfanglab_yara_import\n\nA decomposed HarfangLab YARA imports entry is not a module the YARA language knows.\n\nReported by the analysis engine for an object file: an editor running `opentide lsp start` shows it. `opentide validate` and `opentide lint` are the authority for objects and name the same problem with their own code.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nopentide validate\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nChange the file where the frame points, then run `opentide validate` again.\n",
                "text": "harfanglab_yara_import\n\nA decomposed HarfangLab YARA imports entry is not a module the YARA language knows.\n\nReported by the analysis engine for an object file: an editor running opentide lsp start shows it. opentide validate and opentide lint are the authority for objects and name the same problem with their own code.\n\nExample\n\nopentide validate\n\nHow to fix\n\nChange the file where the frame points, then run opentide validate again."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#harfanglab_yara_import",
              "id": "harfanglab_yara_import",
              "name": "HarfanglabYaraImport",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A decomposed HarfangLab YARA imports entry is not a module the YARA language knows."
