            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "opentide stopped on an exception it did not handle: a bug in opentide, not in your input."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# internal\n\nopentide stopped on an exception it did not handle: a bug in opentide, not in your input.\n\nExit 1, as `failure.code`. Under `--json` the document also carries `failure.crash_log`, the path of a redacted crash log. On stderr, `error[internal]: <Type>: <message>` is followed by that path and a link that opens a pre-filled GitHub issue. Under `opentide mcp start` nothing is written to stdout.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nerror[internal]: RuntimeError: index exploded\n  note: crash log, with credentials and tenant names redacted: ~/.local/state/opentide/crash-<TIMESTAMP>-<PID>.log\n  help: this is a bug in opentide; report it: https://github.com/opentidehq/opentide/issues/new?…\n  help: add --debug to see the traceback\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nOpen the issue from the link, and attach the crash log after reading it: opentide removes the secrets and tenant names it recognises, but not a value it cannot tell is one. Add `--debug` (or `OPENTIDE_DEBUG=1`) to print the traceback on stderr too. See [Troubleshooting](../usage/troubleshooting.md#opentide-stops-with-errorinternal).\n",
                "text": "internal\n\nopentide stopped on an exception it did not handle: a bug in opentide, not in your input.\n\nExit 1, as failure.code. Under --json the document also carries failure.crash_log, the path of a redacted crash log. On stderr, error[internal]: <Type>: <message> is followed by that path and a link that opens a pre-filled GitHub issue. Under opentide mcp start nothing is written to stdout.\n\nExample\n\nerror[internal]: RuntimeError: index exploded\n  note: crash log, with credentials and tenant names redacted: ~/.local/state/opentide/crash-<TIMESTAMP>-<PID>.log\n  help: this is a bug in opentide; report it: https://github.com/opentidehq/opentide/issues/new?…\n  help: add --debug to see the traceback\n\nHow to fix\n\nOpen the issue from the link, and attach the crash log after reading it: opentide removes the secrets and tenant names it recognises, but not a value it cannot tell is one. Add --debug (or OPENTIDE_DEBUG=1) to print the traceback on stderr too. See Troubleshooting (../usage/troubleshooting.md#opentide-stops-with-errorinternal)."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#internal",
              "id": "internal",
              "name": "Internal",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "opentide stopped on an exception it did not handle: a bug in opentide, not in your input."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The command was stopped before it finished: an `opentide serve` client cancelled the run."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# interrupted\n\nThe command was stopped before it finished: an `opentide serve` client cancelled the run.\n\nExit 130, as Ctrl-C at a terminal exits. The command's own cleanup ran first (an open search is stopped, a file being written is not left half-written), so whatever it had not done yet was not done. A command stopped this way prints no document of its own, so `opentide serve` answers with one that carries this code.\n\n## Example\n\n```json\n{\"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\", \"method\": \"$/cancelRequest\", \"params\": {\"id\": 7}}\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nNothing is broken. Run the command again when you need its result; a `validate` or `lint` that was cancelled wrote nothing.\n",
                "text": "interrupted\n\nThe command was stopped before it finished: an opentide serve client cancelled the run.\n\nExit 130, as Ctrl-C at a terminal exits. The command's own cleanup ran first (an open search is stopped, a file being written is not left half-written), so whatever it had not done yet was not done. A command stopped this way prints no document of its own, so opentide serve answers with one that carries this code.\n\nExample\n\n{\"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\", \"method\": \"$/cancelRequest\", \"params\": {\"id\": 7}}\n\nHow to fix\n\nNothing is broken. Run the command again when you need its result; a validate or lint that was cancelled wrote nothing."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#interrupted",
              "id": "interrupted",
              "name": "Interrupted",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The command was stopped before it finished: an `opentide serve` client cancelled the run."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A threat lists CVE identifiers Vulnerability-Lookup does not know."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# invalid_cve\n\nA threat lists CVE identifiers Vulnerability-Lookup does not know.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide validate --check cve`.\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nthreat:\n  cve: [CVE-2024-99999999]\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nCheck each identifier on https://vulnerability.circl.lu and correct or remove the ones it does not find. The check needs network access to the lookup service.\n",
                "text": "invalid_cve\n\nA threat lists CVE identifiers Vulnerability-Lookup does not know.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide validate --check cve.\n\nExample\n\nthreat:\n  cve: [CVE-2024-99999999]\n\nHow to fix\n\nCheck each identifier on https://vulnerability.circl.lu and correct or remove the ones it does not find. The check needs network access to the lookup service."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#invalid_cve",
              "id": "invalid_cve",
              "name": "InvalidCve",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A threat lists CVE identifiers Vulnerability-Lookup does not know."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A reference names an object that does not exist."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# invalid_ref\n\nA reference names an object that does not exist.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide validate` (schema check).\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\ndetection_model: 00000000-0000-4000-8002-DEADBEEF0000   # no such objective\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nPoint the field at an object that exists: `detection_model` takes an objective or signal UUID (`opentide info objectives`), `objective.threats` and `threat.chaining[].vector` take threat UUIDs (`opentide info threats`). When the value is close to the name of an object, `help` names that object's UUID. A reference field is also a vocabulary field, but a dangling value is reported once, under this code.\n",
                "text": "invalid_ref\n\nA reference names an object that does not exist.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide validate (schema check).\n\nExample\n\ndetection_model: 00000000-0000-4000-8002-DEADBEEF0000   # no such objective\n\nHow to fix\n\nPoint the field at an object that exists: detection_model takes an objective or signal UUID (opentide info objectives), objective.threats and threat.chaining[].vector take threat UUIDs (opentide info threats). When the value is close to the name of an object, help names that object's UUID. A reference field is also a vocabulary field, but a dangling value is reported once, under this code."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#invalid_ref",
              "id": "invalid_ref",
              "name": "InvalidRef",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A reference names an object that does not exist."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An object's `metadata.uuid` is not a UUID v4."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# invalid_uuid\n\nAn object's `metadata.uuid` is not a UUID v4.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide validate` (uuid-format check).\n\n## Example\n\n```yaml\nmetadata:\n  uuid: 1234-abcd\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nReplace it with a UUID v4. If other objects already point at the old value, update those references in the same change.\n",
                "text": "invalid_uuid\n\nAn object's metadata.uuid is not a UUID v4.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide validate (uuid-format check).\n\nExample\n\nmetadata:\n  uuid: 1234-abcd\n\nHow to fix\n\nReplace it with a UUID v4. If other objects already point at the old value, update those references in the same change."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#invalid_uuid",
              "id": "invalid_uuid",
              "name": "InvalidUuid",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "An object's `metadata.uuid` is not a UUID v4."
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A `sharing.toml` value has the wrong type."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# invalid_value\n\nA `sharing.toml` value has the wrong type.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by `opentide validate --check sharing-config` and `opentide share`.\n\n## Example\n\n```toml\nenabled = \"yes\"           # must be a boolean\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nUse the type the message names: `true`/`false` for flags, a list of strings for lists.\n",
                "text": "invalid_value\n\nA sharing.toml value has the wrong type.\n\nSeverity: error. Reported by opentide validate --check sharing-config and opentide share.\n\nExample\n\nenabled = \"yes\"           # must be a boolean\n\nHow to fix\n\nUse the type the message names: true/false for flags, a list of strings for lists."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#invalid_value",
              "id": "invalid_value",
              "name": "InvalidValue",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A `sharing.toml` value has the wrong type."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The command ran and failed on what it found."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# issues_found\n\nThe command ran and failed on what it found.\n\nExit 1, as `failure.code`. Set by `validate` (errors, or under `--strict` warnings, or a full run that found no detection objects), `lint --strict` (findings), `info --strict` (load errors) and `deploy` (a rule the platform refused). The findings are in the same document, each with its own code; a `deploy` lists every rule in `results` with its `status`, the platform's message in `error`, and the rules it never reached as `not_attempted`.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nopentide validate\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nFix the findings the command printed. Each one names its code: `opentide explain <code>` says how to fix it. When `validate --strict` failed because it found no detection objects, it printed no finding: check the folder it names (run it inside the workspace or pass `--repo`), or add a first object with `opentide new rule`. A commit hook written by an earlier release fails a new workspace this way: re-run `opentide setup hooks`, whose hook passes `--allow-empty`. After a failed `deploy`, fix the rule on the `✗ failed` line, or the tenant it names, then run the command again: the rules before it were deployed, and the ones marked `not attempted` were not sent.\n",
                "text": "issues_found\n\nThe command ran and failed on what it found.\n\nExit 1, as failure.code. Set by validate (errors, or under --strict warnings, or a full run that found no detection objects), lint --strict (findings), info --strict (load errors) and deploy (a rule the platform refused). The findings are in the same document, each with its own code; a deploy lists every rule in results with its status, the platform's message in error, and the rules it never reached as not_attempted.\n\nExample\n\nopentide validate\n\nHow to fix\n\nFix the findings the command printed. Each one names its code: opentide explain <code> says how to fix it. When validate --strict failed because it found no detection objects, it printed no finding: check the folder it names (run it inside the workspace or pass --repo), or add a first object with opentide new rule. A commit hook written by an earlier release fails a new workspace this way: re-run opentide setup hooks, whose hook passes --allow-empty. After a failed deploy, fix the rule on the ✗ failed line, or the tenant it names, then run the command again: the rules before it were deployed, and the ones marked not attempted were not sent."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#issues_found",
              "id": "issues_found",
              "name": "IssuesFound",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The command ran and failed on what it found."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A KQL control command (.show, .create, ...) is not a detection query; rules cannot run it."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# kql_control_command_unsupported\n\nA KQL control command (.show, .create, ...) is not a detection query; rules cannot run it.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in a KQL query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by `opentide validate` (its `queries` check) and `opentide validate query`; as a warning or a note, style, by `opentide lint` (its `queries` check). An editor running `opentide lsp start` shows both.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nopentide validate query\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run `opentide validate query` (`--file` for one rule or query file) or `opentide lint --check queries` again. The [KQL reference](https://learn.microsoft.com/kusto/query/) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against.\n",
                "text": "kql_control_command_unsupported\n\nA KQL control command (.show, .create, ...) is not a detection query; rules cannot run it.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in a KQL query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by opentide validate (its queries check) and opentide validate query; as a warning or a note, style, by opentide lint (its queries check). An editor running opentide lsp start shows both.\n\nExample\n\nopentide validate query\n\nHow to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run opentide validate query (--file for one rule or query file) or opentide lint --check queries again. The KQL reference (https://learn.microsoft.com/kusto/query/) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#kql_control_command_unsupported",
              "id": "kql_control_command_unsupported",
              "name": "KqlControlCommandUnsupported",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A KQL control command (.show, .create, ...) is not a detection query; rules cannot run it."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The KQL function is a deprecated alias; Microsoft Learn names its replacement."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "# kql_deprecated_function\n\nThe KQL function is a deprecated alias; Microsoft Learn names its replacement.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in a KQL query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by `opentide validate` (its `queries` check) and `opentide validate query`; as a warning or a note, style, by `opentide lint` (its `queries` check). An editor running `opentide lsp start` shows both.\n\n## Example\n\n```text\nopentide validate query\n```\n\n## How to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run `opentide validate query` (`--file` for one rule or query file) or `opentide lint --check queries` again. The [KQL reference](https://learn.microsoft.com/kusto/query/) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against.\n",
                "text": "kql_deprecated_function\n\nThe KQL function is a deprecated alias; Microsoft Learn names its replacement.\n\nReported by the analysis engine in a KQL query, at the place in the query: as an error, a query the platform would reject, by opentide validate (its queries check) and opentide validate query; as a warning or a note, style, by opentide lint (its queries check). An editor running opentide lsp start shows both.\n\nExample\n\nopentide validate query\n\nHow to fix\n\nChange the query where the frame points, then run opentide validate query (--file for one rule or query file) or opentide lint --check queries again. The KQL reference (https://learn.microsoft.com/kusto/query/) describes the syntax and the commands the engine checks against."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://opentide.org/docs/cli/diagnostics#kql_deprecated_function",
              "id": "kql_deprecated_function",
              "name": "KqlDeprecatedFunction",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The KQL function is a deprecated alias; Microsoft Learn names its replacement."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
